window.SD_PRACTICE = [
{"href":"kubernetes/00-index.html","title":"Kubernetes Control Plane: Overview","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Kubernetes Control Plane?","a":"<p>\"The Kubernetes Control Plane is the brain of the cluster — kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, and kube-controller-manager working together to accept requests, store desired state, and continuously reconcile actual cluster state to match it. Worker nodes make up a separate 'data plane' that only talks to the control plane through kube-apiserver, which is why already-running Pods keep working even if the control plane is temporarily down.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/01-kube-apiserver.html","title":"kube-apiserver Deep Dive","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is kube-apiserver?","a":"<p>\"kube-apiserver is the front door of Kubernetes — the single entry point every request (kubectl, Helm, controllers, kubelets) goes through. It authenticates, authorizes, validates, and admission-controls each request, then persists the result into etcd. It holds no state of its own; etcd is the source of truth, which is exactly what lets multiple kube-apiserver replicas run behind a load balancer for high availability.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/02-etcd.html","title":"etcd Deep Dive","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is etcd?","a":"<p>\"etcd is a distributed, strongly consistent key-value store used by Kubernetes as the source of truth for cluster state. The Kubernetes API Server stores resources such as Pods, Deployments, Services, Secrets, and ConfigMaps in etcd. Controllers and the Scheduler use the API Server to observe that state and continuously reconcile the actual cluster with the desired state. etcd uses the Raft consensus algorithm to provide consistency and high availability.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/03-kube-scheduler.html","title":"kube-scheduler Deep Dive","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is kube-scheduler?","a":"<p>\"kube-scheduler is the control-plane component that decides which node an unscheduled Pod should run on. It filters out nodes that can't fit the Pod, scores the remaining candidates, and writes the winning node's name back to the API server as a binding. It never runs anything itself — the kubelet on the chosen node is what actually pulls the image and starts the containers.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/04-kube-controller-manager.html","title":"kube-controller-manager Deep Dive","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is kube-controller-manager?","a":"<p>\"kube-controller-manager is the Kubernetes control-plane component that runs the built-in controllers — Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node, Job, and more — as a single process. Each controller watches the API Server, compares desired state (from etcd) against actual cluster state, and takes corrective action when they diverge. It only ever talks to the API Server — never directly to a kubelet, a worker node, or etcd.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/05-api-request-flow.html","title":"Kubernetes API Request Flow: Auth, AuthZ, Admission","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Happens When a Request Reaches the Kubernetes API Server?","a":"<p>\"Every request to the Kubernetes API server goes through the same pipeline: authentication figures out who you are, authorization (usually RBAC) checks whether you're allowed to do what you're asking, admission control can modify or reject the request, and API validation checks the object against the schema. If it's a write, the API server then persists the object to etcd and controllers react to the change; if it's a read, the API server just returns current state without touching etcd for a new write.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/06-rbac-namespace-scope.html","title":"RBAC: Scoping a Namespace-Only User","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is RBAC Namespace Scoping?","a":"<p>\"RBAC namespace scoping means the Role and RoleBinding you create both belong to one namespace, so the permissions they grant only apply inside that namespace. kube-apiserver's RBAC authorizer only looks at RoleBindings in the namespace a request targets, so a RoleBinding in `frontend` has no effect on requests against `backend`. If you need the same permissions everywhere, or need to grant access to cluster-scoped resources like Nodes, you reach for a ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding instead of duplicating namespaced Roles.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/07-persistent-storage.html","title":"Persistent Storage & Distributed Databases on Kubernetes","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Persistent Storage in Kubernetes (PersistentVolume / PersistentVolumeClaim)?","a":"<p>\"A PersistentVolume represents an actual piece of storage, and a PersistentVolumeClaim is an application's request for storage of a given size and access mode. Kubernetes binds the two together — either matching a pre-created PV or dynamically provisioning one via a StorageClass — so a Pod's data survives the Pod being deleted or rescheduled. For distributed databases, each replica typically gets its own PVC and PV, commonly wired up through a StatefulSet's volumeClaimTemplates, so a recreated Pod reattaches to its own existing storage instead of starting empty. The PV itself only provides durability, not distribution — replication and failover are the database's job.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/08-configmap-secret.html","title":"ConfigMap & Secret: Separating Config from Sensitive Data","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Difference Between a ConfigMap and a Secret?","a":"<p>\"ConfigMap and Secret both let you inject configuration into a Pod as environment variables or mounted files, without hard-coding values into the image. The difference is intent and access control: ConfigMap is for non-sensitive values like feature flags or paths, Secret is for sensitive values like passwords or API keys. That said, a Secret's protection comes from RBAC and encryption at rest that the cluster operator configures — not from the Secret object itself, since its data field is only Base64-encoded by default, not encrypted.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/09-data-plane.html","title":"Data Plane Deep Dive","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Kubernetes Data Plane?","a":"<p>\"The data plane is the worker-node layer that actually executes what the control plane decides — it's made up of the kubelet, the container runtime reached via CRI, the CNI plugin for Pod networking, the CSI driver for storage, and kube-proxy for Service traffic forwarding. All of these run per-node, not per-cluster, and none of them make scheduling or cluster-wide decisions — they carry out what was already decided, and keep running even if the control plane is briefly unavailable.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/10-api-request-lifecycle.html","title":"API Server Request Lifecycle: The Four Gates","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Are the Four Gates of a Kubernetes API Request?","a":"<p>\"Every Kubernetes API request goes through four sequential gates: Authentication figures out who you are, Authorization checks whether you're allowed to do what you're asking, Admission Control evaluates and can mutate or reject the request based on cluster policy, and only after clearing all three does the API Server persist the object to etcd. Each gate is an independent check — passing one gate is no guarantee you'll pass the next.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/11-leader-election.html","title":"Leader Election: HA for Controller Manager & Scheduler","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Leader Election, and Why Do kube-scheduler & kube-controller-manager Need It?","a":"<p>\"Leader election lets Kubernetes run multiple replicas of kube-scheduler and kube-controller-manager for high availability without letting them step on each other. Only one replica — the leader — actually performs reconciliation work at a time; the rest are followers that just watch. It's implemented with a Lease object in etcd: the leader periodically renews it, and if it stops renewing, a follower detects the expired Lease and does an atomic compare-and-swap to become the new leader — so leadership changes safely on a crash, without ever having two leaders active at once.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/12-networking-roadmap.html","title":"Kubernetes Networking Roadmap","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Kubernetes Networking?","a":"<p>\"Kubernetes networking gives every Pod its own IP and lets every Pod reach every other Pod without NAT; a CNI plugin implements that. Because Pod IPs change, a Service gives a group of Pods a stable virtual IP and DNS name, and kube-proxy on each Node programs the rules that forward Service traffic to the current Ready Pods. CoreDNS resolves Service names, Ingress brings external HTTP traffic in through an Ingress controller, and NetworkPolicy restricts which Pods can talk to each other.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/13-control-plane-roadmap.html","title":"Kubernetes Control Plane Roadmap","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Kubernetes Control Plane?","a":"<p>\"The control plane is the brain of Kubernetes. kube-apiserver is the front door every request goes through, etcd stores the desired and current state, kube-scheduler assigns new Pods to Nodes, and kube-controller-manager runs controllers that keep comparing desired state to actual state and fixing the difference. They all coordinate through the API server, and the kubelets on worker Nodes actually run the containers.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/14-request-access-roadmap.html","title":"Request & Access Flow Roadmap","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Kubernetes Request & Access Flow?","a":"<p>\"Every request to the Kubernetes API goes through the same pipeline in the API server. First authentication works out who the caller is, then authorization — usually RBAC — checks whether that identity may perform that verb on that resource, then mutating admission controllers can change the object, the object is schema-validated, validating admission controllers can reject it, and finally it is saved to etcd. Admission only runs on writes, not reads. Failing authentication gives a 401, failing authorization gives a 403.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/15-workload-storage-roadmap.html","title":"Workload Config & Storage Roadmap","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Workload Configuration & Storage in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>\"Kubernetes keeps config and data out of the container image. ConfigMaps hold normal settings and Secrets hold sensitive ones, injected as environment variables or mounted files. For data that must survive restarts, a Pod uses a PersistentVolumeClaim, which binds to a PersistentVolume; with a StorageClass the PV and the underlying disk are provisioned automatically through a CSI driver. Once the disk is attached to the Node, the kubelet mounts it and the config files into the Pod before the container starts.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/16-high-availability-roadmap.html","title":"Control Plane High Availability Roadmap","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Control Plane High Availability?","a":"<p>\"Control plane HA means running the control plane on several Nodes, typically three. The API servers are stateless, so they all run active-active behind a load balancer. etcd runs as a Raft cluster and needs a majority quorum, so three members survive one failure. The scheduler and controller manager run multiple copies but use Lease-based leader election so only one is active, and a standby takes over when the leader's Lease expires.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"kubernetes/17-data-plane-roadmap.html","title":"Kubernetes Data Plane Roadmap","section":"Kubernetes","icon":"☸️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Kubernetes Data Plane?","a":"<p>\"The data plane is the worker Nodes that actually run workloads. On each Node the kubelet watches for Pods assigned to it and runs them by calling the container runtime through CRI, setting up networking through CNI, and mounting storage through CSI. kube-proxy makes Service IPs route to the right Pods. The kubelet sends heartbeats back to the control plane, and if a Node stops reporting, its Pods are eventually evicted and rescheduled elsewhere.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"authentication/00-login-steps.html","title":"Secure Login Implementation Steps","section":"Authentication","icon":"🔐","cards":[{"q":"How Do You Implement a Secure Login?","a":"<p>\"For a secure login I serve everything over HTTPS, store passwords only as salted bcrypt or Argon2 hashes, and verify them in constant time. On success I rotate the session ID and put it in an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie, or issue a short-lived access token with a refresh token. I rate-limit and lock out repeated failures, offer MFA, return a generic 'invalid credentials' message, protect against CSRF, use one-time expiring tokens for password resets, and log and alert on suspicious login activity.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"authentication/01-authentication/01-introduction.html","title":"Authentication Schemes: An Introduction","section":"Authentication","icon":"🔐","cards":[{"q":"What Is an Authentication Scheme?","a":"<p>\"An authentication scheme is how a client proves who it is to a server. For browser users I'd use session cookies or OAuth 2.0 with OpenID Connect for social or enterprise login; for APIs, bearer tokens such as short-lived JWTs; for simple machine access, API keys; and for high-security service-to-service calls, mTLS or HMAC request signing. The trade-offs are statefulness, scalability, and how easily a credential can be revoked — and every scheme must run over HTTPS.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"authentication/01-authentication/03-hashing-process.html","title":"Secure Password Hashing Process","section":"Authentication","icon":"🔐","cards":[{"q":"How Should Passwords Be Stored?","a":"<p>\"I never store passwords, only a hash from a slow password-hashing function like Argon2id or bcrypt. Each user gets a unique random salt so identical passwords produce different hashes and rainbow tables don't work, and the cost factor makes every guess expensive so a stolen database can't be brute-forced quickly. The stored string includes the algorithm, cost, salt, and hash. At login I hash the entered password with the same salt and cost and compare in constant time, and I raise the cost factor as hardware gets faster.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"authentication/02-social-login/google-key-task.html","title":"Google Social Login Checklist","section":"Authentication","icon":"🔐","cards":[{"q":"What Is Social Login (Sign in with Google)?","a":"<p>\"Social login uses OpenID Connect: the user signs in with Google, and my app receives a Google-signed ID token. The mobile app sends that ID token to my backend, and the backend verifies its signature, expiry, and that the audience is my client ID. Then it finds or creates the user and issues my own access and refresh tokens for the rest of the session. Google handles passwords and MFA, and my backend never trusts the client's word about who the user is.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"authentication/02-social-login/google-login.html","title":"Google Sign-In with Credential Manager","section":"Authentication","icon":"🔐","cards":[{"q":"How Does Google Sign-In Work in a Mobile App with a Backend?","a":"<p>\"The Android app uses Credential Manager to get a Google ID token, which is a signed JWT proving who the user is. It sends that token to my backend, which verifies the signature, expiry, and audience with Google's library, then logs the user in or creates their account. The backend then issues its own access and refresh tokens, and the app uses those for all further requests — the Google token is only used once, to establish identity.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/frontend-roadmap.html","title":"Front-End Development Roadmap (Concepts Only)","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Front-End Development?","a":"<p>\"Front-end development is building what runs in the user's browser — HTML for structure, CSS for presentation, and JavaScript for interactivity and talking to APIs. The core skills are understanding how the browser loads and renders a page, building accessible and responsive UIs, organizing the app into components with clear state management, choosing where rendering happens, and shipping fast, tested code through a build pipeline and a CDN.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/04-client-server-model.html","title":"Client-Server Model, End to End","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Client-Server Model?","a":"<p>\"In the client-server model, clients like browsers and mobile apps send requests, and servers process them and send responses. The server centralizes the data and business logic, so everyone shares the same trusted state. A web request goes through DNS, a TCP and TLS connection, and an HTTP request; the server authenticates it, runs logic, uses the database, and returns a response. HTTP is stateless, so cookies, sessions, or tokens identify the user, and at scale the server side grows into load balancers, caches, queues, microservices, and replicated databases.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/04.2-request-flow-interactive.html","title":"Interactive Request Flow","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Happens When You Type a URL and Press Enter?","a":"<p>\"The browser resolves the domain through DNS, opens a TCP connection and does a TLS handshake, and sends the HTTP request. A CDN serves it if it's cached; otherwise it goes through a load balancer, a security layer like rate limiting and a WAF, and an API gateway to the right service. The service checks the user's session or JWT, reads from a cache and falls back to the database, pushes slow work onto a queue, and returns the response. The browser then parses the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript and renders the page.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/04.1-cookies-sessions-jwt.html","title":"Cookies, Sessions & JWT","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Are Cookies, Sessions, and JWTs?","a":"<p>\"HTTP is stateless, so we need a way to remember the logged-in user. Cookies are the transport — the browser stores them and sends them automatically. With server-side sessions, the cookie holds a random ID and the session data lives in Redis or a database, so revocation is instant. With JWTs, the signed token itself carries the user's identity, so any server can verify it without a lookup, but it can't easily be revoked before expiry. In practice I use short-lived access tokens with revocable refresh tokens, stored in HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/browser-javascript-capabilities.html","title":"What In-Browser JavaScript Can & Can't Do","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Browser JavaScript Sandbox?","a":"<p>\"JavaScript's power depends on where it runs. In the browser it's sandboxed: it can manipulate the DOM, handle user events, make network requests, set cookies and use localStorage — but it can't read arbitrary files, run programs, or access the OS, and devices like the camera need the user's permission. The Same Origin Policy stops a script from one site reading another site's pages or responses, and CORS headers are how a server explicitly allows cross-origin reads. The same language in Node.js has no sandbox and can read files and open sockets freely.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/cors.html","title":"CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is CORS?","a":"<p>\"CORS is how a server relaxes the browser's Same Origin Policy. When a page calls an API on a different origin, the browser sends an Origin header, and the server replies with Access-Control-Allow-Origin saying which origins may read the response. For non-simple requests, such as JSON bodies, PUT or DELETE, or custom headers, the browser first sends an OPTIONS preflight to ask permission. It's enforced by the browser, not the server, so it protects users from malicious sites. It doesn't secure your API against curl or other servers.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/01.02-rendering-types.html","title":"Web Rendering & Architecture","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Web Rendering Strategy?","a":"<p>\"Rendering strategy is where and when the HTML is built. CSR builds it in the browser — great for interactive apps, but slower first paint and weaker SEO. SSR builds it on the server per request — fast first content and good SEO, but more server load and a hydration step. SSG builds it at deploy time — fastest and cheapest from a CDN, but only as fresh as the last build; ISR regenerates static pages periodically. I pick per page based on SEO, freshness, personalization, and cost.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/02.1-ssr-vs-csr.html","title":"SSR vs CSR","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Difference Between SSR and CSR?","a":"<p>\"With CSR, the browser gets an empty HTML shell and a JavaScript bundle, runs it, calls the API, and renders the page — so it can be hosted as static files on S3 and a CDN, but first paint and SEO are weaker. With SSR, a server renders the full HTML on each request, so content and SEO are good immediately, but I need servers or serverless compute and pay CPU per request. I use SSR or static generation for public, SEO-sensitive pages and CSR for logged-in, highly interactive apps, often mixed in one Next.js app.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/02.1ssr-vs-csr-v.html","title":"SSR vs CSR (v2)","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Are CSR, SSR, and SSG, in Theory?","a":"<p>\"CSR sends a shell and JavaScript and renders in the browser: cheap to host, but a long path to first content and weaker SEO. SSR renders HTML on the server per request: fast visible content and good SEO, but server cost and a hydration step before interactivity. SSG renders at build time: cheapest and fastest, but not personalized or real-time. Modern approaches like islands, resumability, and React Server Components try to keep SSR's fast first paint while shipping and hydrating less JavaScript.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/graphql/http/03.1graphql-vs-rest.html","title":"GraphQL vs REST","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Difference Between GraphQL and REST?","a":"<p>\"In REST, each resource has its own endpoint and the server fixes the response shape, so a screen that needs related data often makes many requests and over- or under-fetches. In GraphQL, there's one endpoint and a typed schema, and the client asks for exactly the fields it needs in one query, which cuts round trips — especially valuable on mobile. The trade-off is server complexity: I need batching to avoid N+1 database queries, query cost limits, and a different caching strategy, whereas REST gets simple HTTP and CDN caching.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"01-front-end/system-design-roadmap.html","title":"System Design Roadmap — DevOps/SRE → System Design Interviews","section":"Front-End","icon":"🖥️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a System Design Interview?","a":"<p>\"A system design interview tests whether I can take an open-ended problem and design a scalable, reliable system while explaining the trade-offs. I follow a structure: clarify functional and non-functional requirements, estimate scale with quick back-of-the-envelope math, define the API, sketch the high-level architecture, deep-dive into the hardest components, then discuss bottlenecks, failure modes, and trade-offs. I state my assumptions out loud and adjust as the interviewer adds constraints.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/00-learning-path/00-index.html","title":"Database Index","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Database, and Why Does It Get Hard at Scale?","a":"<p>\"A database stores data durably and lets many clients read and write it correctly, usually with ACID transactions on a single node. At scale one node isn't enough, so we replicate for availability and read scaling, which brings replication lag and consistency trade-offs, and we partition or shard for write throughput and data size, which makes cross-partition queries and transactions harder. Understanding databases for system design means knowing those trade-offs — CAP, consistency models, partitioning strategies, and change data capture — and choosing per workload.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/01-foundations/mislanious-terms.html","title":"Miscellaneous Database Terms","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Are Database Integrity Constraints?","a":"<p>\"Integrity constraints are rules the database enforces on every write — NOT NULL, UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, and CHECK. If a write violates one, the database rejects it and rolls the change back, so invalid data can't get in no matter which application, script, or migration wrote it. I validate in the application for good error messages, but I rely on database constraints for correctness, alongside transactions and isolation to handle concurrent changes.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/01a-sql-and-relational-fundamentals/index.html","title":"SQL and Relational Fundamentals","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Relational Database?","a":"<p>\"A relational database stores data in tables with rows and typed columns, each row identified by a primary key, and relationships expressed with foreign keys. Data is normalized so each fact is stored once, which prevents update anomalies, and the database enforces constraints like NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, and foreign keys on every write. SQL lets me join tables back together, aggregate, and filter declaratively, and relational databases give ACID transactions, which is why they're the default for data that must stay correct.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/02-data-modeling-and-selection/how-to-choose-the-database.html","title":"How to Choose the Database","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"How Do You Choose a Database?","a":"<p>\"I choose a database from the access patterns and requirements, not from popularity. I default to a relational database like PostgreSQL when I need transactions, relationships, and flexible queries. I'd pick a key-value store like DynamoDB or Redis for simple lookups at massive scale, a document store for flexible nested records, Cassandra for huge write volumes queried by known keys, Elasticsearch for full-text search, and a time-series database for metrics. I weigh consistency needs, read/write ratio, scale, latency, and what the team can operate.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why do you start from access patterns instead of from the data's shape or the technology?","a":"<p>Because the data model only has to make <em>those operations</em> cheap. The same data (orders) is relational if you need joins and transactions, key-value if you only ever fetch by id, and columnar if you only aggregate it. The shape of the data does not decide; how it is read and written does. Starting from the technology inverts the reasoning and produces the \"we chose MongoDB for money\" class of failures.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is the single requirement that most often forces a relational database?","a":"<p>Multi-row (multi-table) ACID transactions: several rows must change together or not at all — debit one account and credit another, create an order and decrement stock. Most NoSQL families offer atomicity only within one item, document or partition (DynamoDB and MongoDB have added bounded multi-item transactions, at a cost), so this requirement eliminates them early.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why should a search engine like Elasticsearch never be the source of truth?","a":"<p>It has no transactions, a write becomes visible only after the refresh interval (near-real-time, about a second), its durability guarantees are weaker than a database's, and its whole design assumes the index can be dropped and rebuilt from the data it indexes. Keep the truth in a database and feed the index through change-data-capture; when the index drifts, reindex.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why does the decision tree ask about blobs first?","a":"<p>Because files, images and videos belong in object storage (S3) regardless of every other answer. Putting a 5 MB image in a database row inflates backups, replication and cache memory for no benefit and cannot be served by a CDN. Only the metadata (owner, size, URL, timestamp) goes in the database, so asking about blobs first removes the largest bytes from the rest of the decision.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What happens internally when you run a monthly revenue report on the OLTP primary, and why does checkout slow down?","a":"<p>The report scans millions of rows across many pages, pulling them through the buffer pool. Those pages evict the hot order and customer pages that checkout needs; the next checkout queries miss the cache and go to disk, so their latency rises. At the same time the scan competes for CPU and IOPS. Cause → effect: analytical scan → working set evicted → OLTP cache misses → p99 spike. The fix is a read replica, then a columnar warehouse fed by CDC.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"You are told \"we need to scale writes\". Walk through what you check before changing the database.","a":"<p>First, which table and pattern: often one append-only event table produces most WAL; partition it by time and move the stream to a purpose-built store rather than touching the transactional core. Second, write amplification: unused indexes multiply every insert; drop them. Third, batching and connection pooling. Fourth, instance size. Only if a single node's sustained write ceiling is genuinely reached for the transactional workload itself do you consider sharding or distributed SQL — and that is a migration project with its own worksheet.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Relational vs. document store for a product catalog — how do you decide?","a":"<p>Ask how the data is read and related. If a product is fetched whole with its variants and attributes, attributes vary by category, and cross-product queries are few, a document store (or PostgreSQL with a <code>JSONB</code> attributes column) avoids a 200-column sparse table and a migration per attribute. If pricing, inventory and orders join against the catalog transactionally, keep it relational and put only the variable attributes in <code>JSONB</code>. The deciding factors are read-whole vs. joined, and how often the shape changes.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What does \"design the partition key before choosing DynamoDB or Cassandra\" mean in practice?","a":"<p>In those stores the partition key decides which node holds the data and therefore how load spreads. For every access pattern you must be able to name a key that is high-cardinality (many distinct values) and evenly accessed, and that the query already knows. A key like <code>date</code> puts all of today's writes on one partition; <code>user_id</code> for a celebrity makes one hot partition. If you cannot name such a key for each pattern, you will either need a query table per pattern or you have the wrong store.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"When is distributed SQL (Spanner, CockroachDB) the right call, and what does it cost you?","a":"<p>When you need relational semantics and ACID transactions <em>and</em> either writes exceed what one node can sustain or you need strong consistency across regions. It costs commit latency: every write waits for a consensus round across replicas (tens of milliseconds across zones, more across regions), plus higher price and operational novelty. For a single-region workload that fits one primary with replicas, it is paying for a problem you do not have.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How do you keep several stores consistent in a polyglot system, and why not write to all of them in the request?","a":"<p>Dual writes fail half-way: the database commit succeeds and the search index write fails, and nothing reconciles them. Instead, write only to the source of truth and publish each committed change through change-data-capture (reading the WAL, e.g. Debezium) or a transactional outbox table, to a stream that updates the derived stores asynchronously. Derived stores become eventually consistent by a measurable lag, and can be rebuilt from the source when they drift.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"What changes in the decision at 100× scale, and what does not?","a":"<p>What changes: the single relational primary stops being the answer for high-ingest streams and for analytics; those move to wide-column / time-series and warehouse stores fed by CDC; the cache becomes a cluster; consistency is documented per pattern (strong for money, eventual for feeds); cost per request and cross-region transfer become criteria. What does not change: transactional data still wants ACID; the procedure is still access patterns → consistency → volume → latency → flexibility → operations; and every added store must be justified by a measured pattern.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How would you argue against a proposal to move the order service from PostgreSQL to Cassandra \"for scale\"?","a":"<p>Ask for the measurement: which pattern's p99 regressed and what is the write rate against one node's ceiling? Point out the family-level mismatch: orders need multi-row transactions and finance asks ad-hoc questions, both of which Cassandra lacks. Offer the cheaper levers — partition the event tables, add replicas, cache, archive — and the right split: keep orders relational, move the append-only event stream (the likely real bottleneck) to Cassandra or a warehouse. Then propose the <code>revisit_when</code> thresholds that would reopen the question.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Production: after a Redis node is replaced, thousands of users are logged out and some lose shopping carts. What do you check and what do you change?","a":"<p>Check <code>INFO persistence</code>: AOF probably off and RDB snapshot hours old, so the in-memory data was the only copy. Check whether carts and sessions have any durable source. Change: treat Redis as a cache — write carts through to a durable store (PostgreSQL or DynamoDB) and keep sessions reconstructible from a signed token or a durable table; if Redis must hold state, enable AOF with <code>appendfsync everysec</code>, replicate, and rehearse a restore. The selection lesson: anything that must survive a restart needs a durable source of truth, which step 3 of the procedure would have flagged.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Production: DynamoDB throws throttling errors although the table's consumed capacity is far below provisioned. What is happening?","a":"<p>A hot partition. Capacity is divided across partitions, so one partition receiving most traffic throttles while the table as a whole looks idle. Confirm with CloudWatch <code>ThrottledRequests</code> alongside low <code>ConsumedWriteCapacityUnits</code>, and Contributor Insights to find the key. Fix by redesigning the key: add a write-sharding suffix and fan reads across the shards, or choose a higher-cardinality key; cache the hot key if it is a read. Adaptive capacity softens but does not fix a single giant key.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Production: search shows products that were deleted an hour ago while the catalog page is correct. Where do you look?","a":"<p>The search index is a derived copy, so the pipeline from the primary to Elasticsearch is lagging or broken. Check the CDC / outbox consumer's lag (Kafka consumer group lag), the indexer's error logs, and compare document counts between primary and index. Fix the pipeline, then reindex the affected range from the primary. Add an alert on consumer lag and a periodic count-reconciliation job so the next drift is caught in minutes, not by users.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Production: checkout p99 spikes every day at 09:00 for twenty minutes; CPU on the database primary is high and the buffer hit ratio drops at the same time. Diagnose.","a":"<p>Dashboards or scheduled reports are running analytical scans on the OLTP primary. Confirm in <code>pg_stat_statements</code>: long aggregate queries with high <code>shared_blks_read</code> at 09:00. Short term, point the reporting connections at a read replica. Longer term, CDC the tables into a warehouse or ClickHouse so analytics has its own engine. Record in the worksheet that the workload is OLTP + OLAP and must stay split.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"05-database/02-data-modeling-and-selection/factors.html","title":"Database Performance Factors","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Determines Database Performance?","a":"<p>\"Database performance comes down to latency and throughput, and it's limited by whichever resource is the bottleneck: query efficiency and indexes, CPU, memory and cache hit ratio, disk IOPS, connections, or locks. I measure first — slow query log, EXPLAIN ANALYZE, percentiles, and resource metrics — then fix the actual bottleneck with indexes, query changes, caching, pooling, read replicas, or scaling, and re-measure.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/02-data-modeling-and-selection/scratch-note-polyglot-persistence.html","title":"Scratch Note: Polyglot Persistence","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Polyglot Persistence?","a":"<p>\"Polyglot persistence means using different databases for different workloads in one system — for example PostgreSQL for transactions, Redis for caching, Elasticsearch for search, and a warehouse for analytics. I keep strongly consistent, transactional data in a relational database as the source of truth, and move workloads that need different query patterns or scale to specialized stores, synced with change data capture or events. The cost is operational complexity and keeping the copies consistent.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/02a-schema-design-and-migrations/index.html","title":"Schema Design and Migrations","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Schema Design and a Schema Migration?","a":"<p>\"I design the schema from the application's access patterns — the hot reads and writes — choosing correct data types and letting the database enforce constraints. I normalize by default and denormalize only for measured read-heavy paths. Schema changes go through versioned migrations, and I keep them backward compatible with expand and contract: add the new structure, backfill it in batches, switch the code, then drop the old structure in a later release, so rolling deploys and rollbacks never break.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/03-querying-and-performance/01A-Database-basic.html","title":"PostgreSQL Views Explained","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a View in PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>\"A view is a saved query that behaves like a virtual table — it stores no data, and every query against it runs the underlying SQL on the live tables, so it's always up to date. I use views to reuse complex joins and to restrict what columns or rows a role can see. A materialized view stores the query result physically, so reads are fast, but the data is only as fresh as the last REFRESH — good for expensive dashboards and reports.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/03-querying-and-performance/08-indxing.html","title":"Indexing","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Database Index?","a":"<p>\"An index is a separate sorted data structure, usually a B-tree, that lets the database find rows by a column's value without scanning the whole table — turning a linear scan into a logarithmic lookup. I index the columns used in WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY on hot queries, and use composite indexes in the order the queries filter. The trade-off is that every index costs storage and slows down every insert and update, so I only add indexes that real queries need, and I combine them with denormalization and caching for read-heavy paths.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/03a-transactions-and-concurrency/index.html","title":"Transactions and Concurrency","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Database Transaction?","a":"<p>\"A transaction groups several operations so they succeed or fail together, with ACID guarantees: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability. Isolation levels trade correctness for concurrency — Read Committed, Repeatable Read, Serializable. Databases like Postgres use MVCC so readers don't block writers. For read-modify-write updates like inventory or balances, I use an atomic update, SELECT FOR UPDATE, or optimistic concurrency with a version column to prevent lost updates, and I acquire locks in a consistent order to avoid deadlocks.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/03b-query-plans-and-optimization/index.html","title":"Query Plans and Optimization","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Query Plan?","a":"<p>\"A query plan is how the database decides to execute a SQL query — index or full scan, join order, and join algorithm — based on its statistics. I use EXPLAIN ANALYZE to see the real plan with timings and row counts, look for sequential scans on big tables, bad row estimates, and expensive joins, then fix it with the right index, a rewritten query, updated statistics, or by removing N+1 queries in the application — and I always measure before and after.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/04-distributed-database-fundamentals/lecture-2.html","title":"Lecture 2","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Difference Between ACID and BASE?","a":"<p>\"ACID means transactions are atomic, consistent, isolated, and durable — the model of relational databases, ideal for money and inventory. BASE means basically available, soft state, eventually consistent — the model of many distributed NoSQL stores, which stay available and fast but let replicas be briefly out of date. It's a spectrum, not a switch: many NoSQL databases let me tune consistency per request with quorums or read and write concerns, so I pick the guarantee each piece of data actually needs.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/04-distributed-database-fundamentals/04-cap-theorem-questions.html","title":"CAP Theorem Questions","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the CAP Theorem?","a":"<p>\"CAP says that during a network partition, a distributed database must choose between consistency — every read sees the latest write or gets an error — and availability — every request gets an answer, possibly stale. Since partitions can't be ruled out, the real choice is CP or AP behaviour during a partition. I choose per use case: CP for things like balances, locks, and leader election; AP for things like feeds, carts, and counters. PACELC extends this to the latency-versus-consistency trade-off when there's no partition.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/04a-database-security-and-access-control/index.html","title":"Database Security and Access Control","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"How Do You Secure a Database?","a":"<p>\"I secure a database in layers. Each service gets a least-privilege role, and multi-tenant data uses row-level security. Connections use TLS with certificate verification; storage and backups are encrypted, with extra column-level encryption for the most sensitive fields. Credentials live in a secrets manager and are rotated, never in code. Every query is parameterized to prevent SQL injection, sensitive data is masked in non-production copies, and access is audited and reviewed regularly.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/05-scaling-and-partitioning/01-Database-Basic.html","title":"Database Basics: Partitioning & Data Distribution","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Data Distribution in Databases?","a":"<p>\"Data distribution is spreading data across machines. Partitioning splits the data into pieces — horizontally by rows or vertically by columns — and sharding is horizontal partitioning across different servers, which scales storage and writes. Replication copies the same data to multiple servers for availability and read scaling. I pick a partitioning strategy — range, list, hash, or composite — from the access patterns, and usually replicate each shard too.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/05-scaling-and-partitioning/02-partiontion.html","title":"Partitioning","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Partitioning, and How Is It Different From Sharding?","a":"<p>\"Partitioning splits a large table into smaller pieces — by rows horizontally or by columns vertically — so queries can skip irrelevant data and old data is easy to drop. Sharding is horizontal partitioning across different servers, which scales storage and writes beyond one machine, but makes cross-shard joins, transactions, and rebalancing harder. The most important decision is the partition key: it should match the main access pattern and spread load evenly, otherwise you get hot partitions.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/05-scaling-and-partitioning/03-horizintal-scaling.html","title":"Horizontal Scaling","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Difference Between Vertical and Horizontal Scaling?","a":"<p>\"Vertical scaling makes one database server bigger — simple, no code changes, and transactions stay easy — but it has a hard ceiling, gets expensive, and is still a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling adds more servers — read replicas for reads and sharding for writes — which removes the ceiling and adds fault tolerance, but brings replication lag, cross-shard queries, rebalancing, and operational complexity. I usually optimize and scale vertically first, add read replicas and caching next, and shard only when writes or data size require it.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/05a-connection-management-and-database-proxies/index.html","title":"Connection Management and Database Proxies","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Connection Pooling?","a":"<p>\"Connection pooling reuses a set of open database connections instead of opening one per request, because opening connections is slow and each one costs the database memory, so it has a hard connection limit. I keep pools small, sized from measurements, and make sure the sum across all service instances stays under max_connections. With many instances or serverless functions, I add a proxy like PgBouncer or RDS Proxy to multiplex many client connections onto a few database connections. I also set timeouts, health checks, and leak detection.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/05a-connection-management-and-database-proxies/postgresql-connection-troubleshooting.html","title":"PostgreSQL Connection & Query Troubleshooting Guide","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"How Do You Troubleshoot \"Too Many Connections\" in PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>\"When Postgres says too many connections, I query pg_stat_activity to see who holds them: count by state, then group by client IP, user, and application name. Many active connections mean slow queries or real load; many idle ones mean oversized pools across too many instances; and 'idle in transaction' means the application isn't closing transactions, which also holds locks. I fix the cause — pool sizes, leaks, slow queries — and add a pooler like PgBouncer, rather than just raising max_connections or killing sessions.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/06a-backup-recovery-and-disaster-recovery/index.html","title":"Backup, Recovery, and Disaster Recovery","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Are Backup, Recovery, and Disaster Recovery?","a":"<p>\"Backups are copies of the data stored separately so I can restore after deletion or corruption; with WAL archiving I can do point-in-time recovery to just before a mistake. I design to two numbers: RPO, how much data we can lose, which decides backup frequency, and RTO, how long we can be down, which decides restore speed and automation. Replication handles node failure but copies mistakes instantly, so it's not a backup. And I regularly test restores and rehearse the DR runbook, including a full-region failure if the business needs it.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"05-database/07-observability-capacity-and-operations/index.html","title":"Observability, Capacity, and Operations","section":"Database","icon":"🗄️","cards":[{"q":"What Does Database Observability and Operations Involve?","a":"<p>\"For a database I monitor p95 and p99 query latency, connection and pool usage, replication lag, lock waits and deadlocks, disk and IOPS, and the slow query log, and I alert well before hard limits. I track growth trends to plan capacity and pick the right lever — bigger instance, read replicas, pooling, or partitioning — and load-test before big events. Operationally, I schedule maintenance like vacuum and analyze, plan upgrades with a rollback path, and investigate incidents by correlating the symptom's time window across these metrics.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-system/distributed-systems.html","title":"Distributed Systems","section":"Distributed Systems","icon":"🌐","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Distributed System?","a":"<p>\"A distributed system is a group of independent machines that communicate over a network and work together so they look like a single system to the user. We build them to scale beyond one machine, to stay available when a machine fails, and to be closer to users. The cost is that the network is unreliable: messages can be delayed or lost and nodes can fail independently, so we need replication, consensus, and careful handling of consistency and partial failure.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-system/01-consensus-algorithms.html","title":"Consensus Algorithms: Raft & Paxos","section":"Distributed Systems","icon":"🌐","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Consensus Algorithm?","a":"<p>\"A consensus algorithm lets a cluster of nodes agree on one value, or one ordered log of values, even when some nodes fail. Raft and Paxos both do it with majority quorums: a write is committed only after a majority of nodes accept it, and because any two majorities overlap, a committed value can't be lost. Raft elects a leader that handles all writes and replicates them to followers. A cluster of N nodes survives ⌊(N−1)/2⌋ failures, which is why clusters like etcd run with 3 or 5 nodes.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-system/02-consistent-hashing.html","title":"Consistent Hashing","section":"Distributed Systems","icon":"🌐","cards":[{"q":"What Is Consistent Hashing?","a":"<p>\"Consistent hashing places both servers and keys on a hash ring, and each key goes to the next server clockwise. With plain hash-mod-N, adding one server remaps almost every key; with the ring, adding or removing a server only moves about 1/N of the keys — the ones in that server's slice. Virtual nodes put each server on the ring many times so load stays even. It's used in Cassandra and DynamoDB partitioning, cache client sharding, and load balancer affinity.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-database/index.html","title":"Replication & Distributed Data — Solution Architect Roadmap (Part 1)","section":"Distributed Database","icon":"🧬","cards":[{"q":"What Is Replication in a Distributed Database?","a":"<p>\"Replication means keeping copies of the same data on multiple nodes and streaming every change between them, for availability, read scaling, disaster recovery, and locality. The core choices are the topology — single-leader, multi-leader, or leaderless — and the timing — synchronous for no data loss or asynchronous for low latency. Asynchronous replication introduces lag and stale reads, so I pick a consistency model per use case, use quorums or consensus for leader election, and plan failover so we never end up with split-brain.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-database/02-replication-mechanism.html","title":"Replication Mechanism — The 4 Dimensions (Part 2)","section":"Distributed Database","icon":"🧬","cards":[{"q":"What Is Database Replication?","a":"<p>\"Replication keeps copies of the same data on multiple database nodes by shipping each change from one node to the others. We do it for high availability, read scaling, and disaster recovery. I describe any replication setup by four choices: the topology — single-leader, multi-leader, or leaderless; the timing — synchronous for zero data loss or asynchronous for low latency; the payload — statements, rows, logical records, or physical WAL; and the delivery — log shipping or change data capture.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-database/03-pacelc-interview-masterclass.html","title":"PACELC — Senior Interview Masterclass","section":"Distributed Database","icon":"🧬","cards":[{"q":"What Is PACELC?","a":"<p>\"PACELC extends CAP. If there's a network partition, a distributed database chooses between availability and consistency; else, during normal operation, it chooses between latency and consistency. The 'else' part matters most day to day, because strong consistency means waiting for other replicas on every write, which costs latency. Cassandra and DynamoDB default to PA/EL, systems like HBase and ZooKeeper are PC/EC, and in many databases you can tune this per operation — for example Cassandra with ONE versus QUORUM.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-database/04-wal-vs-binlog.html","title":"WAL vs Binlog — Storage Engine vs Server Log","section":"Distributed Database","icon":"🧬","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Difference Between a WAL and a Binlog?","a":"<p>\"The WAL is the storage engine's physical redo log: every change is written to it before the data pages are modified, so after a crash the database can replay it and not lose committed transactions. The binlog is MySQL's logical log of row changes or statements, written at commit time, and it's used for replication and point-in-time recovery. In MySQL the two are kept consistent with an internal two-phase commit. PostgreSQL doesn't have a separate binlog — its WAL is used for both crash recovery and replication.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"distributed-database/05-replication-lag.html","title":"Replication Lag — Causes, Measurement, Mitigation","section":"Distributed Database","icon":"🧬","cards":[{"q":"What Is Replication Lag?","a":"<p>\"Replication lag is how far a replica is behind the leader — the time between a write committing on the leader and becoming visible on the replica. It comes from asynchronous replication, and it's usually caused by the replica being slow to apply changes — disk I/O, single-threaded apply, or heavy read queries — not by the network. Lag causes users to not see their own writes or see data go backwards, and it can mean data loss on failover. I handle it by routing a user's reads to the leader right after they write, only reading from replicas under a lag threshold, and alerting on lag.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"sqs/01-sqs.html","title":"SQS Overview","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"What Is Amazon SQS?","a":"<p>\"SQS is AWS's fully managed message queue. Producers send messages, SQS stores them redundantly across Availability Zones, and consumers poll, process, and delete them. It decouples services and absorbs traffic spikes, because the producer doesn't wait for the consumer. A visibility timeout hides a message while it's being processed and makes it reappear if the consumer fails, and a dead-letter queue catches messages that keep failing. Standard queues give high throughput with at-least-once delivery; FIFO queues give strict ordering and deduplication.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why put a queue between two services?","a":"<p>The producer returns immediately and doesn't fail when the consumer is slow or down; the queue buffers spikes and consumers scale independently.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What does the visibility timeout do?","a":"<p>Hides a received message from other consumers while one works on it; if it isn't deleted in time, it reappears for another attempt.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's a dead-letter queue for?","a":"<p>Isolating messages that failed <code>maxReceiveCount</code> times, so they stop retrying forever and can be inspected and replayed.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Standard vs. FIFO — when do you choose FIFO?","a":"<p>When order per entity matters or duplicates are costly, and throughput fits FIFO limits; parallelise with many message group IDs.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Visibility timeout vs. retention period?","a":"<p>Visibility is a short per-receive lock (who processes now); retention is the message's whole lifetime in the queue.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do you make a consumer idempotent?","a":"<p>Record a processed key (order ID or message deduplication ID) atomically with the side effect — e.g. a unique constraint — and skip if it already exists.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Does SQS guarantee exactly-once?","a":"<p>No. Standard is at-least-once; FIFO deduplicates within 5 minutes, but end-to-end exactly-once still requires idempotent consumers.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How would you autoscale consumers?","a":"<p>Target a backlog per consumer (visible messages ÷ consumers) and an age-of-oldest-message ceiling; cap max consumers by downstream capacity.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How does a poison message affect a FIFO queue?","a":"<p>It blocks its message group (later messages in that group wait) until it's dead-lettered — so set maxReceiveCount and a DLQ.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Customers get two confirmation emails for one order. Why?","a":"<p>The consumer sent the email but crashed or timed out before deleting; the message reappeared. Make sending idempotent (record sent per order) and size the visibility timeout correctly.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Black Friday: the queue holds 2 million messages and the oldest is 40 minutes old. What do you do?","a":"<p>Scale consumers within downstream limits, batch operations, check for a slow dependency, prioritise critical work (separate queues), and keep an eye on retention.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"The DLQ suddenly has 5,000 messages. Next steps?","a":"<p>Inspect samples for a common error, correlate with deploys/outages, fix the cause, then redrive from the DLQ in a controlled way.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"sqs/02-queus-methord.html","title":"Queue Methods","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"What Is AMQP, and How Do Messaging Protocols Differ?","a":"<p>\"AMQP is an open binary protocol for talking to message brokers like RabbitMQ. Producers publish to an exchange, the exchange routes messages to queues by routing key, and consumers acknowledge each message after processing so the broker can redeliver on failure or send it to a dead-letter queue. I'd pick AMQP for reliable work queues with retries, MQTT for lightweight IoT pub/sub, Kafka's protocol for replayable event streams, webhooks for simple event notifications to third parties, and gRPC for direct service-to-service calls.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"sqs/use-case.html","title":"SQS Use Cases","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"Why Use a Message Queue in System Design?","a":"<p>\"A message queue sits between services so the producer can hand off work and move on, and consumers process it at their own pace. That decouples services, so one being down doesn't break the other; it absorbs traffic spikes as a backlog; it keeps messages safe if a consumer crashes; and it lets me scale by adding consumers. I use it for anything that doesn't need to happen within the user's request — emails, video processing, order fulfilment — and as the backbone of event-driven systems.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"sqs/03-kafka-fundamentals.html","title":"Kafka Fundamentals","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"What Is Kafka?","a":"<p>\"Kafka is a distributed, durable, append-only log. Topics are split into partitions spread across brokers; producers append messages, and consumers read by offset without deleting anything, so many consumer groups can read the same stream independently and replay it. Ordering is guaranteed per partition, so I choose a partition key like the order ID. Partitions are replicated, and I use acks=all with idempotent producers for durability. I'd pick Kafka over SQS when I need high throughput, fan-out, or replay.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"sqs/04-event-sourcing-and-cqrs.html","title":"Event Sourcing & CQRS","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"What Are Event Sourcing and CQRS?","a":"<p>\"Event sourcing stores every change as an immutable event and rebuilds current state by replaying them, which gives a full audit trail and time travel; snapshots keep replay fast. CQRS separates the write model from the read models, so each side can be optimized independently. They're often combined: commands append events, projections update read views, and queries read the views — which means reads are eventually consistent. I'd use them for audit-heavy domains like ledgers, not for simple CRUD.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"sqs/05-microservice-resilience-patterns.html","title":"Microservice Resilience Patterns","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"What Are Microservice Resilience Patterns?","a":"<p>\"Resilience patterns stop one failing service from taking down the whole system. I set timeouts on every call, retry transient failures with exponential backoff and jitter, and only retry idempotent operations. A circuit breaker fails fast when a dependency is down and lets it recover; bulkheads give each dependency its own thread or connection pool. For operations spanning services I use a saga with compensating transactions instead of two-phase commit, and I put shared concerns in an API gateway at the edge or a service mesh between services.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"sqs/project/project.html","title":"Multiprotocol Messaging Starter: Project Guide","section":"Messaging & Queues","icon":"📨","cards":[{"q":"What Are Retries and Dead-Letter Queues?","a":"<p>\"When a consumer fails to process a message, I retry it a limited number of times, ideally with exponential backoff, because many failures are transient. If it still fails after the maximum attempts, I move it to a dead-letter queue so the poison message doesn't block or waste the workers, and I alert on the DLQ and replay messages once the cause is fixed. RabbitMQ supports this natively with dead-letter exchanges; in Kafka I build it with retry and DLQ topics.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"storage/storage-type.html","title":"Storage Types","section":"Storage","icon":"💾","cards":[{"q":"What Are File, Object, and Block Storage?","a":"<p>\"Block storage is a raw virtual disk attached to one server — low latency and high IOPS — so I use it for databases and VM boot volumes, like EBS. File storage is a shared file system with folders and paths over NFS or SMB, good for shared drives and content many servers read and write, like EFS. Object storage keeps data as objects with metadata and a key in a flat bucket, accessed over HTTP; it scales almost without limit and is very durable and cheap, so I use it for images, videos, backups, and data lakes, like S3, usually behind a CDN.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"caching/caching-roadmap.html","title":"Caching Roadmap","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Caching Strategy for a System?","a":"<p>\"I decide caching layer by layer: static assets at the CDN with long TTLs and versioned URLs, hot read-heavy objects in Redis with cache-aside, and a small in-process cache only for data that is almost never written. For each cache I state the eviction policy — usually LRU or LFU with a <code>maxmemory</code> limit — a TTL that matches how stale the data may be, and how writes invalidate it. Then I protect the database from mass misses: request coalescing for hot keys, TTL jitter against mass expiry, and negative caching or a Bloom filter against requests for keys that don't exist.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"caching/00-caching-and-cdn-fundamentals.html","title":"Caching & CDN Fundamentals","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is Caching?","a":"<p>\"Caching stores a copy of frequently read data in a faster layer — browser, CDN, Redis, or process memory — so most reads skip the database or origin. The most common pattern is cache-aside: check the cache, on a miss read the database and populate the cache, and on a write update the database and invalidate the key. Every cache trades some staleness for speed, so I always state the strategy, the eviction policy, the TTL, and how the data gets invalidated.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why does caching work at all?","a":"<p>Reads vastly outnumber writes and access is skewed (a few keys get most reads), so a small, fast copy answers most requests.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Eviction vs. invalidation?","a":"<p>Eviction removes entries because memory is full; invalidation removes them because the data changed. A good eviction policy doesn't prevent stale reads.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"no-cache vs. no-store?","a":"<p><code>no-cache</code> may be stored but must be revalidated before use; <code>no-store</code> must never be stored.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"In cache-aside, why delete the key on write instead of updating it?","a":"<p>Concurrent writers can update the cache out of order, leaving a permanently wrong value. Deleting makes the next read reload from the source of truth.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"When would you choose write-behind, and what's the risk?","a":"<p>When write latency or bursty writes are the bottleneck (counters, analytics). Risk: data in the cache but not yet flushed is lost on a crash — needs a durable log/queue.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Redis or Memcached for a leaderboard?","a":"<p>Redis — sorted sets give ranked inserts and range queries natively; Memcached only stores blobs.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do you prevent a cache stampede on a hot key?","a":"<p>Request coalescing (one recompute, others wait), early probabilistic refresh, TTL jitter, and stale-while-revalidate.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Stampede vs. hot key — why do they need different fixes?","a":"<p>A stampede is many misses on an expired key (fix the refill); a hot key overloads one shard even when warm (fix distribution: local caching or replicating the key).</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How do you invalidate caches when many services write the same data?","a":"<p>Publish change events (CDC or a queue) that every cache subscribes to, or use versioned keys; accept eventual consistency within a bounded TTL.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"After a Redis restart the database CPU hits 100% and the site falls over. Why, and how do you prevent it next time?","a":"<p>The DB was sized for cache-hit traffic; a cold cache sends every read to it. Prevent with stampede protection, gradual traffic ramp or cache warm-up, DB capacity headroom, and Redis replicas/persistence.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A user saw another user's account page. What happened?","a":"<p>A personalized response was cached by a shared cache (CDN/proxy) — missing <code>private</code>/<code>no-store</code> or a cache key without the user. Purge immediately, fix headers and keys.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"You deployed a JS fix but many users still run the old bundle. What do you do?","a":"<p>Purge the CDN path now; long term, fingerprint filenames so every deploy is a new URL with a long TTL and the HTML references the new name.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/01-types-of-cache.html","title":"Types of Cache","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Types of Cache Are There?","a":"<p>\"Caches exist at every layer: the browser and mobile app cache responses per user; DNS and the CDN cache at the network edge; a reverse proxy or API gateway caches whole responses in front of the app; the application caches objects in its own memory or in a shared store like Redis; and the database keeps hot pages in its buffer pool. The closer a cache is to the user, the more it saves on a hit, but the harder it is to invalidate — so I pick the layer per data type: static assets at the CDN, shared hot objects in Redis, and tiny rarely-changing data in-process.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What's the difference between a private and a shared cache?","a":"<p>A private cache (browser) serves one user; a shared cache (CDN, proxy) serves many, so it must never store one user's private response under a key others can hit.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why doesn't \"buff/cache\" memory in free mean a leak?","a":"<p>It's the OS page cache using idle RAM for file blocks; the kernel gives it back when applications need memory.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What does a CDN origin shield do?","a":"<p>All edge PoPs fetch through one designated PoP, so the origin sees one miss per object instead of one per PoP.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Distributed cache vs. in-process cache?","a":"<p>One consistent shared copy with a network hop vs. many microsecond-fast copies that go stale independently. Combine them as a near cache for hot keys.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is a negative cache and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>Caching \"not found\" for a short TTL, so repeated lookups for missing keys (often attacks — cache penetration) don't reach the database.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Is a read replica a cache?","a":"<p>Not exactly: it holds all data and answers any query, updated by replication; a cache holds a subset for key lookups. Both serve possibly stale reads.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why did MySQL remove its query cache?","a":"<p>Any write to a table invalidated all its cached queries, and a global lock serialized access — on write-heavy, multi-core workloads it cost more than it saved.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How do you invalidate the L1 of a near cache on every instance?","a":"<p>Short L1 TTLs, pub/sub invalidation messages, or Redis client-side caching (<code>CLIENT TRACKING</code>) where Redis pushes invalidations for keys a client read.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why does a session store need a different eviction policy from a cache?","a":"<p>The sessions are the primary copy; evicting one logs a user out. Use <code>noeviction</code> or volatile-only eviction, replication and possibly persistence.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"After a database failover, one Java service keeps failing for an hour. Others recovered. Why?","a":"<p>The JVM cached the old IP (<code>networkaddress.cache.ttl</code>) or DNS TTLs were long; the service keeps connecting to the dead primary. Lower the JVM DNS TTL and record TTLs.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A fixed price shows correctly on some page loads and wrongly on others. Where do you look?","a":"<p>Per-instance in-process caches — some instances refreshed, others haven't. Add invalidation or shorten the L1 TTL.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Redis CPU is maxed on one node by a single celebrity profile key. Fix?","a":"<p>Hot key: add a near cache (in-process L1 with a short TTL) or replicate that key across nodes with random suffixes.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/01a-browser-caching.html","title":"Browser Caching","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is Browser Caching?","a":"<p>\"Browser caching lets the browser reuse responses it has already downloaded, controlled by the server's <code>Cache-Control</code> and validator headers. A fresh response within <code>max-age</code> is used with no request at all; a stale one is revalidated with <code>If-None-Match</code>, and the server answers <code>304 Not Modified</code> with no body if nothing changed. Because I can't purge users' browsers, I give static assets content-hashed filenames with a one-year <code>immutable</code> TTL, and serve the HTML with <code>no-cache</code> so it is revalidated each time and always points to the latest asset URLs. Private data is <code>private</code> or <code>no-store</code>.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What's the difference between a fresh hit and a revalidation?","a":"<p>A fresh hit uses the stored response with no request. A revalidation sends a conditional request; a <code>304</code> means \"unchanged, reuse your copy\".</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why can't you just purge a bad file from users' browsers?","a":"<p>The cache is on millions of devices you don't control. You can only change URLs (fingerprinting) or wait for expiry.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What does immutable add to max-age?","a":"<p>It tells the browser not to revalidate even on reload, saving 304 round trips for content that can never change at that URL.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"ETag vs. Last-Modified?","a":"<p>ETag is an exact version identifier (usually a content hash); Last-Modified has one-second resolution and changes on redeploys with identical bytes. Prefer content-hash ETags.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why is Vary: Cookie bad for caching?","a":"<p>Every distinct cookie value is a separate variant — effectively one cache entry per user — so shared caches almost never hit.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why don't public CDN copies of jQuery help across sites any more?","a":"<p>Browsers partition the HTTP cache by top-level site to stop timing-based history leaks, so a copy cached on site A isn't reused on site B.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What's heuristic freshness and why is it dangerous?","a":"<p>With no explicit lifetime but a Last-Modified date, browsers may cache for ~10% of the time since modification — days or weeks you never chose. Always send Cache-Control.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Which Service Worker strategy for HTML, and why?","a":"<p>Network-first with cache fallback: users get the latest version online and still work offline. Cache-first HTML can pin users to an old app.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Fingerprinted filenames vs. ?v= query strings?","a":"<p>Filenames are safer: some proxies/CDN configs ignore or strip query strings from the cache key, so a <code>?v=</code> bump may not bust everything.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"After a release, some users see a blank page. Diagnose.","a":"<p>Their cached HTML references asset files the deploy removed → 404 on JS. Restore old assets; make HTML <code>no-cache</code> and keep previous assets for a while.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A shared library computer showed the previous user's account page via Back. Fix?","a":"<p>Mark sensitive pages <code>no-store</code> and send <code>Clear-Site-Data: \"cache\"</code> on logout.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Your HTML has ETags but users never get 304s behind the load balancer. Why?","a":"<p>Each server generates different ETags (e.g. inode-based), so validators never match. Use content-hash ETags or identical artifacts.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/01b-cdn-caching.html","title":"CDN Caching","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is CDN Caching?","a":"<p>\"A CDN caches my responses in edge locations close to users, so most requests never reach my origin. Users are routed to the nearest PoP by anycast or DNS; on a miss the PoP fetches from the origin, often through an origin shield so the origin sees one request per object, and caches it for the TTL set by <code>s-maxage</code>. I keep the cache key minimal to get a high hit ratio, version static asset URLs so I never need to purge them, purge by tag for content that changes unpredictably, and mark personalised responses <code>private</code> so the CDN never shares them. The metric I watch is the hit ratio and origin offload.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Anycast vs. GeoDNS for routing users to a PoP?","a":"<p>Anycast: all PoPs announce one IP and BGP routes to the nearest (also spreads DDoS). GeoDNS: DNS returns a nearby PoP's IP based on the resolver's location — which may not be the user's.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What does an origin shield do?","a":"<p>Funnels all edge misses through one cache location, so the origin sees roughly one request per object per TTL.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why use s-maxage?","a":"<p>To cache longer at the CDN (which you can purge) than in browsers (which you can't).</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's the difference between the cache key and what's forwarded to the origin?","a":"<p>Some inputs must reach the origin without splitting the cache. But if the origin's response depends on a forwarded-but-unkeyed input, the CDN serves one variant to everyone.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why is soft purge often better than hard purge?","a":"<p>Objects are marked stale rather than deleted, so the edge can keep serving them while refreshing — avoiding a miss stampede on the origin.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How does micro-caching help a dynamic homepage?","a":"<p>A 1–5 s TTL collapses thousands of identical requests per second into ~1 origin request per PoP per TTL, while content stays nearly live.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Explain web cache poisoning.","a":"<p>An attacker sends an unkeyed header the origin reflects into the response (e.g. <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code>); the poisoned response is cached and served to everyone. Don't let unkeyed inputs affect responses; strip unknown headers at the edge.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Explain web cache deception.","a":"<p>Tricking the CDN into caching a private page via a static-looking URL (<code>/account/profile.css</code>). Cache by explicit rules/content type, 404 unknown paths, and honour origin <code>Cache-Control</code>.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why can request collapsing hurt for uncacheable URLs?","a":"<p>Waiters queue behind a request whose response can't be shared, serializing them. CDNs mark such URLs hit-for-pass so later requests go straight to the origin.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"CDN hit ratio dropped from 90% to 20% after a release. What do you check?","a":"<p>New cookies or <code>Set-Cookie</code> on cacheable responses, new query parameters in the key, <code>Vary</code> changes, missing <code>Cache-Control</code>, or a cache-policy change.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Attackers bypass your CDN's WAF and hit the origin directly. Fix?","a":"<p>Lock the origin to the CDN: Origin Access Control for S3, security groups limited to CDN IP ranges, or a secret header verified at the origin.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A price update takes 24 hours to appear. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The origin sends no caching headers and the CDN default TTL (e.g. 24 h) applies. Send explicit <code>s-maxage</code>, and tag-purge on price changes.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/01c-proxy-and-gateway-caching.html","title":"Reverse Proxy & API Gateway Caching","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is Reverse-Proxy Caching?","a":"<p>\"A reverse-proxy cache like Nginx or Varnish sits in front of the app servers and stores full HTTP responses, so repeated requests are answered without running application code. It's most useful for public pages and APIs with many identical requests — even micro-caching a dynamic page for one second collapses a traffic spike into one backend call per second. I configure the cache key carefully, lock concurrent misses so only one request hits the backend, and serve stale content while updating or when the backend errors. API gateways offer the same per-endpoint response caching.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What does a reverse-proxy cache save that a CDN doesn't?","a":"<p>Application work for all traffic reaching your datacenter — including CDN misses from many PoPs and clients that bypass the CDN.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why does Nginx refuse to cache some responses by default?","a":"<p>Responses with <code>Set-Cookie</code> or <code>Cache-Control: private/no-store</code>, and non-GET/HEAD methods, may be user-specific or unsafe to share.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is micro-caching?","a":"<p>Caching dynamic pages for 1–5 seconds so bursts of identical requests cost one render per TTL.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What do proxy_cache_lock and proxy_cache_use_stale updating do together?","a":"<p>Lock lets one request per key refresh; use_stale + background update serves the old copy to everyone else meanwhile — stampede protection with zero waiting.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Varnish purge vs. ban?","a":"<p>Purge removes one object now; ban is a lazily-applied rule that invalidates every matching object when next requested.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why can't proxies cache typical GraphQL traffic, and what helps?","a":"<p>Queries go as POST to one URL. Persisted queries over GET make them cacheable; otherwise cache in the resolver layer.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does adding proxy instances lower the hit ratio, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Each instance caches independently, so the same URL misses on each. Route URLs by consistent hash to proxies, or use a single shared cache tier.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"When is caching at the API gateway dangerous?","a":"<p>For user- or tenant-specific endpoints keyed by path only — the first caller's response is served to everyone. Include identity in the key or don't cache.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Does an AWS ALB cache responses?","a":"<p>No. Put CloudFront (or a proxy cache) in front for response caching.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"A news homepage at 3,000 req/s takes the app down during breaking news. Quick fix?","a":"<p>Micro-cache the homepage for 1–2 s at the proxy with lock and stale-while-updating; the backend renders it about once per second per proxy.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"After enabling proxy caching, users report seeing the wrong cart. Why?","a":"<p>Cart pages were cached without bypassing on the session cookie. Purge, add <code>proxy_cache_bypass</code>/<code>proxy_no_cache</code> on the cookie, and mark responses <code>private</code>.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"The app was down for 40 minutes but nobody was paged. How did that happen?","a":"<p><code>proxy_cache_use_stale error timeout</code> kept serving stale pages. Alert on upstream errors and the STALE share, not only on client-facing status codes.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/01d-database-caching.html","title":"Database Caching","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is Database Caching?","a":"<p>\"Databases cache internally: the buffer pool keeps hot data and index pages in RAM — ideally the whole working set — and it's always consistent. I check its hit ratio before anything else. On top of that, materialized views precompute expensive queries, read replicas spread read load, and for hot reads that are still too slow or too frequent, I add Redis in front using cache-aside with TTLs and invalidation on write. The key difference is that the database's own cache never serves stale data, while an external cache can, so I only add one where some staleness is acceptable.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why is the buffer pool never stale while Redis can be?","a":"<p>The engine manages the buffer pool transactionally as part of every read and write; Redis is a separate copy the database knows nothing about.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"View vs. materialized view?","a":"<p>A view runs its query on every read; a materialized view stores the result and is as fresh as its last refresh.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What buffer cache hit ratio would you expect for OLTP?","a":"<p>Typically above 99%; lower suggests the working set doesn't fit or queries scan too much.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is PostgreSQL shared_buffers usually ~25% of RAM and InnoDB's pool 50–75%?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL reads through the OS page cache (double buffering), so the rest of RAM still caches data; InnoDB uses O_DIRECT and bypasses the OS cache, so it needs the larger pool.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why did MySQL remove the query cache?","a":"<p>Any write to a table invalidated all its cached results, and a global lock limited concurrency — net negative on write-heavy, multi-core workloads.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do databases stop one big scan from flushing hot pages?","a":"<p>InnoDB inserts new pages at an LRU midpoint (old sublist); PostgreSQL uses small ring buffers for large sequential scans.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What does DAX give you, and what doesn't it?","a":"<p>Microsecond, API-compatible read-through/write-through caching for DynamoDB; reads are eventually consistent — strongly consistent reads pass through to DynamoDB.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Is a read replica a cache?","a":"<p>No: it holds all data and answers any query, updated by replication — but it offloads reads and shares the staleness problem (replication lag).</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why prefer caching objects by ID over caching query results?","a":"<p>A write to row 42 maps to one key (<code>order:42</code>); it's hard to know which cached query results it affects.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"p99 doubled after a failover and slowly recovered over 30 minutes. Explain.","a":"<p>The new primary's buffer pool was cold; queries read from disk until the working set was reloaded. Prewarm on failover candidates.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Your team wants Redis because \"the DB is slow\". What do you check first?","a":"<p>Slow queries and plans (missing indexes), buffer cache hit ratio, connection pooling, and whether a materialized view or replica fits — then Redis for hot objects if still needed.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A sales dashboard query takes 8 s and runs every minute. Fix?","a":"<p>Materialize it (view or rollup table) with an index, refresh on a schedule (<code>CONCURRENTLY</code>), and query the precomputed table.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/01e-dns-os-hardware-caching.html","title":"DNS, OS & Hardware Caching","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is DNS Caching?","a":"<p>\"DNS answers are cached at every step — browser, OS, runtime, and recursive resolvers — for the TTL on the record, so lookups are fast and authoritative servers aren't overloaded. The consequence is that DNS changes propagate only as caches expire: before a planned migration I lower the TTL to about 60 seconds a day ahead, and I don't rely on DNS alone for instant failover because some clients cache longer than the TTL. Below that, the OS page cache keeps recently used file data in RAM, and CPU caches keep recently used memory close to the cores — which is why sequential access patterns are so much faster.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"List the caches a DNS lookup can hit.","a":"<p>Browser host cache → OS stub resolver → language runtime (e.g. JVM) → recursive resolver → authoritative server on a full miss.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why does free show almost no free memory on a healthy server?","a":"<p>The kernel uses idle RAM as page cache and reclaims it on demand; watch <code>available</code>.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is negative DNS caching?","a":"<p>Resolvers cache NXDOMAIN answers for a time from the zone's SOA, so a name looked up before it existed stays \"missing\" for that period.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is Kafka fast without a big internal cache?","a":"<p>It writes to and reads from the OS page cache and uses <code>sendfile</code> zero-copy; consumers that keep up read from RAM.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does iterating an array beat a linked list even at the same big-O?","a":"<p>Arrays use whole 64-byte cache lines and prefetching; linked lists chase pointers to scattered memory, missing the cache constantly.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What's the ndots:5 problem in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>Names with fewer than five dots are tried against every search domain first, multiplying DNS queries for external names; use FQDNs with a trailing dot or tune ndots.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Explain false sharing and its fix.","a":"<p>Two threads write different variables on the same cache line, so coherence keeps invalidating each core's copy. Pad or align per-thread data to separate lines.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why does InnoDB use O_DIRECT?","a":"<p>It manages its own buffer pool and avoids double-caching pages in the OS page cache.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why must TLS session ticket keys be shared across servers?","a":"<p>So a client resuming on a different server behind the load balancer can still be decrypted; otherwise resumption fails and full handshakes return.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"You'll move a site to a new IP tomorrow; the A record TTL is 86,400. Plan it.","a":"<p>Today: lower TTL to 60–300 s. After 24 h: change the record; keep the old server serving for at least the old TTL. Afterwards raise the TTL again.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"After a database failover, one service fails for 30 minutes while others recover in a minute. Why?","a":"<p>That service (often JVM-based) or its connection pool cached the old IP longer than the DNS TTL. Configure runtime DNS TTL and pool max-lifetime/validation.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A container is OOM-killed though the app's heap is small. What could memory metrics be including?","a":"<p>Page cache charged to the container's cgroup inflates usage figures; check working-set vs. cache, then real causes (native memory, leaks). See Memory Profiling.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/02-in-memory-caching-strategies.html","title":"In-Memory Caching Strategies","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Caching Strategy?","a":"<p>\"A caching strategy defines who fills the cache and how writes keep it in sync. My default is cache-aside: the app reads the cache, on a miss reads the database and stores the result with a TTL, and on a write it updates the database and then deletes the cache key. Write-through writes cache and database together so the cache is always current but writes are slower; write-behind writes only the cache and flushes to the database asynchronously — fastest, but data can be lost if the cache dies; write-around skips the cache on writes; and refresh-ahead reloads hot keys before they expire.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Cache-aside vs. read-through?","a":"<p>In cache-aside the application loads on a miss; in read-through the cache/library calls a loader — shorter app code and often built-in miss coalescing.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is cache-aside resilient to a cache outage?","a":"<p>Reads fall back to the database; the cache is optional on the read path.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's write-around good for?","a":"<p>Write-once, read-rarely data — it avoids filling the cache with entries nobody reads.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why delete instead of update the cache on write?","a":"<p>Two writers can update DB and cache in opposite orders, leaving the cache wrong until TTL. Deleting makes the next read load the latest DB value.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why update the DB first, then delete the cache?","a":"<p>Deleting first lets a concurrent reader reload and cache the old row before the DB write lands.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What does refresh-ahead cost?","a":"<p>Reloads for keys that may not be read again — database capacity spent on predictions.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Is \"DB first, then delete\" perfectly consistent?","a":"<p>No: a reader can miss, read the old row, stall, then write it to the cache after the writer's delete. TTLs bound it; delayed double delete or versioned keys narrow it further.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"What is the dual-write problem and how does CDC solve it?","a":"<p>DB write + cache delete aren't atomic; a crash or failed delete leaves stale data. CDC tails the DB log and invalidates for every committed change, regardless of which writer made it.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"When is write-behind acceptable?","a":"<p>High write rates where a small loss window is fine (counters, metrics), or when a durable log sits behind the cache.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Design caching for a product price that changes rarely but must never be wrong at checkout.","a":"<p>Cache-aside for browsing with delete-on-write via CDC and a short TTL; at checkout read the price from the database (or validate it) rather than trust the cache.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A user edits their profile and the page still shows the old name. Fix?","a":"<p>Ensure delete happens after the DB commit; add read-your-writes (serve the writer from the DB briefly) or write-through for profile data.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Video view counts hammer the database with UPDATEs. Design it.","a":"<p>Write-behind: <code>INCR</code> in Redis, track dirty IDs, flush batched increments every few seconds; accept a small loss window or enable AOF.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/03-in-memory-eviction-policies.html","title":"In-Memory Eviction Policies","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Cache Eviction Policy?","a":"<p>\"An eviction policy decides what to drop when the cache is full. LRU drops the entry not used for the longest time and is the usual default because recent use predicts future use; LFU drops the least-used entry and is better when popularity is stable; FIFO and random are cheap but ignore usage. Eviction handles memory, TTL handles freshness, so I set both. In Redis I set <code>maxmemory</code> and a policy like <code>allkeys-lru</code> or <code>allkeys-lfu</code>, because the default <code>noeviction</code> makes writes fail when memory is full.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Eviction vs. expiration?","a":"<p>Eviction is triggered by memory pressure regardless of age; expiration is triggered by a TTL regardless of memory.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is LRU the usual default?","a":"<p>Temporal locality — recently used keys are likely to be used again — and it's simple, O(1) and adapts quickly.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"How do you implement LRU in O(1)?","a":"<p>Hash map (key → node) plus a doubly linked list ordered by recency: move to head on access, evict from tail.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"LRU vs. LFU?","a":"<p>LRU trusts recency (adapts fast, fooled by scans); LFU trusts frequency (resists scans, slow to adapt, needs decay).</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why can MRU beat LRU?","a":"<p>For cyclic scans larger than the cache, LRU always evicts the key needed next; MRU keeps part of the cycle resident.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How does Redis expire keys?","a":"<p>Lazily on access plus active sampling ~10 times per second, repeating when more than 25% of a sample is expired.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Is Redis LRU exact?","a":"<p>No: it samples a few keys (<code>maxmemory-samples</code>) using a per-key last-access clock and evicts the best candidate from a pool — approximate, but cheap.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How does W-TinyLFU work?","a":"<p>New keys enter a small LRU window; leaving it, they compete with the main SLRU's victim using frequencies from a Count-Min sketch — one-time scan keys lose and are dropped.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"What does InnoDB's midpoint insertion protect against?","a":"<p>Scans: new pages enter the \"old\" sublist and only move to \"young\" if read again after a delay, so one scan can't flush hot pages.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"After setting volatile-lru, writes start failing with OOM. Why?","a":"<p>Most keys have no TTL, so there are no eviction candidates; volatile-* then behaves like noeviction. Add TTLs or use allkeys-*.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"The hit ratio drops from 95% to 60% every night at 2 a.m. Diagnose.","a":"<p>A batch job scans many cold keys and pollutes LRU. Switch to allkeys-lfu / scan-resistant policy or isolate the batch workload.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Redis is at maxmemory with a high eviction rate and a low hit ratio. Next steps?","a":"<p>Check key sizes and TTLs (<code>--bigkeys</code>, keys without TTL), remove waste, then add memory or shard — the working set doesn't fit.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"caching/04-in-memory-cache-problems.html","title":"In-Memory Cache Problems","section":"Caching & CDN","icon":"🧊","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Thundering Herd Problem in Caching?","a":"<p>\"A thundering herd is when a hot cache key expires and thousands of concurrent requests miss together and all hit the database to rebuild the same value, which can overload it. I prevent it by letting only one request rebuild the key — a per-key lock or single-flight — while the others wait or get the stale value; by refreshing hot keys early or in the background before they expire; and by adding random jitter to TTLs so keys don't expire at the same moment. Related problems are cache penetration, where requests ask for keys that don't exist, fixed with negative caching or a Bloom filter, and cache avalanche, where many keys or the whole cache fail at once.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Thundering herd vs. avalanche?","a":"<p>Herd: many requests for one expired key. Avalanche: misses across many keys at once (mass expiry or cache outage).</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is cache penetration?","a":"<p>Requests for keys that exist neither in the cache nor the DB, so every one reaches the database. Fix with negative caching, Bloom filters, validation, rate limits.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why add jitter to TTLs?","a":"<p>Keys written together would expire together; jitter spreads expiry so misses don't arrive in one wave.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"How does stale-while-revalidate stop a stampede without blocking?","a":"<p>A soft expiry inside the value triggers one background refresh while everyone keeps getting the stale value until the hard TTL.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does a lock alone not fully fix the herd?","a":"<p>Waiters still need a plan — blocking adds latency and holds threads; they should serve stale or retry briefly, and the lock needs a timeout longer than a rebuild.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do you handle a hot key?","a":"<p>Local L1 cache with a 1–2 s TTL, or replicate the key across shards with random suffixes, or spread reads over replicas.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why are big keys dangerous in Redis?","a":"<p>Commands run on a single thread; reading, writing or deleting a huge value blocks everything else. Split values, use <code>UNLINK</code>, iterate with <code>*SCAN</code>.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Can cache-aside be strongly consistent?","a":"<p>Not for free: races (stale reader re-caching after delete), failed deletes and replica lag all exist. TTLs bound it; double delete, versions, leases and CDC narrow it; strong consistency needs all reads/writes through one serialising component.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How does consistent hashing reduce cold-start pain?","a":"<p>Adding or removing a node remaps only ~1/N of keys instead of nearly all, so most keys stay warm.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Exactly one hour after a deploy the database melts down. Why?","a":"<p>The deploy warmed the cache with identical TTLs (EX 3600); everything expired together — an avalanche. Add jitter and stale-while-revalidate.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A bot requests random user IDs and the database CPU climbs. Fix?","a":"<p>Validate IDs, add a Bloom filter of existing IDs, cache negative results briefly, and rate-limit clients with many misses.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"During a flash sale one Redis shard hits 100% CPU. Diagnose and fix.","a":"<p>Hot key on that shard (the sale product). Confirm with <code>--hotkeys</code> / per-shard metrics; add an in-process L1 or replicate the key across shards.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"traffic/00-load-balancing.html","title":"Load Balancing","section":"Traffic Management","icon":"🚦","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Load Balancer?","a":"<p>\"A load balancer sits in front of a pool of servers and spreads incoming traffic across them, so I can scale horizontally and survive instance failures. It uses an algorithm like round robin or least connections to pick a backend, and health checks to stop sending traffic to instances that fail. An L4 balancer forwards connections by IP and port; an L7 balancer understands HTTP and can route by path or header and terminate TLS. For multiple regions, DNS or anycast picks the region first, and a local load balancer picks the instance.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why does a load balancer improve availability, not just capacity?","a":"<p>Because health checks remove failing instances from rotation: an instance dies → it fails its checks → after the threshold it's marked unhealthy → traffic goes only to the rest. Without health checks it only adds capacity.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"When would you choose L4 over L7?","a":"<p>When the traffic isn't HTTP, or raw throughput/latency matters more than content-aware routing. Choose L7 when you need path/host/header routing, TLS termination, cookie affinity or per-request retries.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's the difference between load balancing and rate limiting?","a":"<p>A load balancer decides <em>where</em> a request goes; a rate limiter decides <em>whether</em> it's allowed. A balancer alone won't stop one abusive client from overloading every backend.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is round robin a poor choice for long-lived or uneven requests, and what would you use?","a":"<p>Round robin ignores current load, so a backend still busy with slow requests keeps getting new ones. Least connections (or power-of-two-choices at large scale) sends work to the least-busy backend.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do health-check interval, timeout and threshold interact?","a":"<p>Detection time ≈ interval × unhealthy threshold. Too aggressive: one blip removes healthy hosts (flapping). Too lax: dead hosts get traffic for a long time. Passive checks on real traffic shorten detection.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What do sticky sessions cost you?","a":"<p>The pinned instance is a single point of failure for its users, load becomes uneven, and scale-in breaks sessions. Prefer stateless backends with session state in Redis or a signed token.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Your gRPC service sits behind an L4 load balancer and one backend is overloaded. Why?","a":"<p>gRPC multiplexes many requests over one long-lived HTTP/2 connection; an L4 balancer balances connections, so each client's whole stream lands on one backend. Use an L7 (HTTP/2-aware) balancer, client-side balancing, or periodic connection recycling.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why can a \"deep\" health check cause a total outage?","a":"<p>If every instance's check includes \"can I reach the database\", a database blip fails all checks at once, the balancer marks every backend unhealthy and returns 503 for everything — even requests that didn't need the database. Keep LB checks instance-local; surface dependency health elsewhere.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How do GSLB and local load balancing divide the work?","a":"<p>GSLB (DNS or anycast) picks the region or datacenter; the local LB picks the instance within it. Different problems at different layers — DNS failover is bounded by TTLs, anycast by routing convergence.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Every deploy causes a burst of 502s. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether instances are deregistered with connection draining before shutdown, whether new instances receive traffic only after passing readiness/health checks, and the deploy batch size vs. remaining capacity. Fix with draining + health-gated rolling deploys.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Users report being logged out at random after you scaled from 1 to 3 instances. Why?","a":"<p>Sessions live in instance memory; requests now land on instances that don't hold the session. Short-term: sticky sessions. Proper fix: shared session store or stateless tokens.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"After adding a new instance, it immediately shows high latency and errors. Why?","a":"<p>Least-connections sends a flood to the empty newcomer while its caches/JIT are cold, or it was added before it was ready. Use readiness checks and slow-start (gradual ramp-up) for new targets.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"traffic/01-rate-limiting-and-throttling.html","title":"Rate Limiting & Throttling","section":"Traffic Management","icon":"🚦","cards":[{"q":"What Is Rate Limiting?","a":"<p>\"Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a time window, so one caller can't overload a shared service or run up costs. I'd usually use a token bucket, which allows short bursts but enforces an average rate, enforced at the API gateway per API key, with Redis holding the counter so every instance sees the same count. Rejected requests get a 429 with a Retry-After header, and clients retry with jittered backoff.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why does a shared service need rate limiting if it already autoscales?","a":"<p>Autoscaling lags and costs money, and shared dependencies (database, third-party APIs) don't scale with it. A limit stops one client from consuming capacity — or budget — meant for everyone.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What status code and headers should a rejected request get?","a":"<p><code>429 Too Many Requests</code> with <code>Retry-After</code>, plus <code>X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset</code> so clients can self-throttle.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's the boundary problem with a fixed window?","a":"<p>A client can send the full limit at the end of one window and again at the start of the next, getting nearly 2× the limit in a short span. Sliding windows fix it.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Token bucket vs. leaky bucket — when do you use each?","a":"<p>Token bucket for inbound API limits: allows bursts up to capacity, enforces an average. Leaky bucket for outbound calls to a fragile downstream: smooths bursts into a constant rate.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Where would you enforce a \"3 password-reset emails per hour\" rule, and why not at the gateway?","a":"<p>In the service that sends the email, keyed by account: it's a business rule tied to one operation and needs the account identity, while the gateway handles coarse per-key/IP limits.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How is rate limiting different from backpressure and circuit breaking?","a":"<p>Rate limiting enforces a policy regardless of downstream health; backpressure reacts to a downstream falling behind; a circuit breaker stops calling a dependency that is failing.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do you make a distributed limiter race-free?","a":"<p>Shared counter in Redis, with check-and-increment as one atomic operation — <code>INCR</code> + TTL in a Lua script, or a Lua token bucket. Separate read and write calls let concurrent requests on different instances both pass.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Redis is down. Should the limiter fail open or closed?","a":"<p>It depends on what the limit protects. Fail open keeps the API available but unprotected (common for general API limits, ideally with a local fallback limit); fail closed suits security-critical limits like login attempts.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"At millions of requests per second, a Redis call per request is too expensive. What then?","a":"<p>Approximate: each instance counts locally and syncs to the shared store periodically, or gets a slice of the global quota. You trade exactness for no per-request network hop.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"A customer says they are limited to 100/min but are actually getting ~400/min through. What's wrong?","a":"<p>Probably four instances (or four Nginx nodes) each enforcing 100/min with local counters. Move to a shared atomic counter.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"After a short outage, your API is hit by a wave of traffic that trips every limit, then another wave. Why?","a":"<p>Clients retry immediately and in sync (thundering herd), aligned to the same <code>Retry-After</code>. Clients need jittered exponential backoff; server-side, consider spreading <code>Retry-After</code> values.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A whole university complains they get 429s while barely using the API. Why?","a":"<p>The limit is keyed by IP and thousands of users share one NAT address. Key on user or API key; keep IP limits only as a loose outer layer.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"networking/00-networking-fundamentals.html","title":"Networking Fundamentals","section":"Networking","icon":"🔌","cards":[{"q":"What Happens on the Network When a Client Calls a Server?","a":"<p>\"Before an HTTPS request is answered, the client resolves the hostname with DNS, opens a TCP connection with a three-way handshake, negotiates encryption with a TLS handshake, and then sends the HTTP request. Each step costs round trips, so systems reuse connections, cache DNS, resume TLS sessions, and use CDNs to cut latency. TCP gives reliable ordered delivery, UDP trades that for speed, HTTP/2 multiplexes requests over one connection, HTTP/3 moves to QUIC over UDP, and WebSockets or gRPC streaming are used when the server needs to push data.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"How many round trips does a cold HTTPS request cost before the response starts?","a":"<p>DNS (0 if cached, several if cold) + 1 for TCP + 1 for TLS 1.3 (2 for TLS 1.2) + 1 for the HTTP request/response. QUIC merges TCP and TLS into one.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"When would you pick UDP over TCP?","a":"<p>When a late packet is worthless and you'd rather skip it than wait — live voice/video, games, DNS queries — or when you build your own transport on top (QUIC).</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's the difference between TLS and HTTPS?","a":"<p>TLS is the encryption and identity layer; HTTPS is ordinary HTTP sent over a TLS connection.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why do connection pools and keep-alive matter so much?","a":"<p>They pay the TCP and TLS handshakes once and reuse the connection for many requests. Without them every request pays 2–3 extra RTTs plus TLS CPU, and high-rate clients exhaust ephemeral ports with TIME_WAIT sockets.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How does a DNS TTL affect failover?","a":"<p>Resolvers keep serving the cached answer until the TTL expires, so a region failover via DNS takes effect only as caches expire. Short TTLs speed failover but increase resolver load and lookup latency.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"WebSockets or polling for a live notifications feed?","a":"<p>WebSockets (or SSE) if the server must push promptly — one long-lived connection instead of repeated requests. Plan for reconnects, heartbeats, LB idle timeouts and affinity, since the connection is stateful.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does HTTP/2 still suffer head-of-line blocking, and how does HTTP/3 fix it?","a":"<p>All HTTP/2 streams share one TCP connection, and TCP delivers bytes in order, so one lost packet stalls every stream. QUIC orders each stream independently over UDP, so loss only delays the affected stream.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"What's the risk of TLS 1.3 0-RTT?","a":"<p>Early data can be captured and replayed, so the server might execute it twice. Accept only idempotent requests as 0-RTT.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why might gRPC be a bad fit for a browser-facing API?","a":"<p>Browsers can't drive raw HTTP/2 streams, so gRPC needs a gRPC-Web proxy; the binary protobuf payloads also aren't human-readable or cacheable like plain HTTP/JSON. It shines for internal service-to-service calls.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"An API is fast from the US but slow from Asia; server time is identical. What do you check?","a":"<p>Round trips: <code>curl -w</code> from Asia to split DNS / TCP / TLS / TTFB. Long RTTs multiplied by setup handshakes dominate. Fix with connection reuse, TLS termination at a nearby edge/CDN, TLS 1.3, and caching.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Some clients fail TLS with \"unable to get local issuer certificate\" but browsers work. Why?","a":"<p>The server isn't sending the intermediate certificate. Browsers often fetch or cache intermediates; strict clients (curl, mobile apps, other services) don't. Serve the full chain; verify with <code>openssl s_client</code>.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A service starts failing outbound calls with \"cannot assign requested address\" under load. Why?","a":"<p>Ephemeral port exhaustion: it opens a new TCP connection per request, leaving thousands in TIME_WAIT. Use keep-alive and a connection pool.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"observability-security/00-observability-fundamentals.html","title":"Observability Fundamentals","section":"Observability, Security & Ops","icon":"🛡️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Observability?","a":"<p>\"Observability is being able to explain what a system is doing from the signals it emits — metrics, logs, and traces. Metrics tell me something is wrong and when, traces show which service in the request path was slow, and structured logs tied together by a correlation ID show exactly why. I define reliability as SLIs and SLOs with an error budget, and I alert on user-facing symptoms like error rate or latency, not on every possible cause.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What is each of metrics, logs and traces best at?","a":"<p>Metrics: cheap trends and alerting (\"something is wrong, when\"). Traces: where time went across services (\"which hop\"). Logs: exact detail of one event (\"why\").</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"SLI vs. SLO vs. SLA?","a":"<p>SLI is the measurement, SLO the internal target, SLA the external (often contractual) promise — usually looser than the SLO.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why prefer percentiles over averages for latency?","a":"<p>Averages hide the tail: a fine mean can coexist with a terrible p99 that a meaningful share of users experience.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why does Prometheus pull instead of services pushing?","a":"<p>Service discovery decides what should exist; a target that is down shows as a failed scrape (<code>up == 0</code>) instead of silently missing data, and services don't need to know where the monitoring lives.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How does a trace stay connected across five services?","a":"<p>Context propagation: each hop passes the trace ID and parent span ID (W3C <code>traceparent</code>) to the next; every service must participate, or the chain breaks there.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is an error budget used for?","a":"<p>It's 100% − SLO. While budget remains, teams ship; when it's spent, they pause risky changes and prioritise reliability — turning reliability vs. velocity into a measurable decision.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why is a user_id metric label dangerous?","a":"<p>Each label value combination is a new time series; a million users means a million series, exhausting memory and slowing queries. Put user-level detail in logs and traces.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Head-based vs. tail-based trace sampling?","a":"<p>Head-based decides at the start (cheap, but may drop the interesting slow request); tail-based decides after the trace completes, keeping slow/error traces, at the cost of buffering spans.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why alert on symptoms rather than causes?","a":"<p>Symptoms (error rate, SLO burn) map to user pain and catch causes you didn't predict; cause alerts multiply, flap and fire without user impact, producing alert fatigue.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Checkout p99 jumped from 300 ms to 4 s at 15:10. Walk through your investigation.","a":"<p>Confirm in metrics which route/service and exact start time → check deploys/config changes then → open slow traces from 15:10 to find the slow span (e.g. payment API call) → read that span's logs via trace ID for the error/timeout → roll back or mitigate, confirm p99 recovers.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Your Prometheus server keeps getting OOM-killed after a release. What do you suspect?","a":"<p>The release added a high-cardinality label. Find the metrics with the most series, drop or relabel the label, and move that detail to logs.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"The team gets 40 pages a week and has started ignoring them. What do you change?","a":"<p>Audit alerts: delete non-actionable ones, turn cause alerts into dashboard panels or tickets, add <code>for:</code> durations, and replace thresholds with SLO burn-rate alerts that each have an owner and runbook.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"observability-security/01-application-security-fundamentals.html","title":"Application Security Fundamentals","section":"Observability, Security & Ops","icon":"🛡️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Application Security?","a":"<p>\"Application security is making sure only the right users and systems can reach the right data and actions. In practice I start from the OWASP Top 10: enforce authorization on the server for every request, use parameterized queries to prevent injection, encrypt data in transit with TLS and at rest with properly managed keys, keep secrets in a secrets manager instead of code, patch dependencies, and log and alert on security events. I threat-model the design up front so security flaws aren't baked into the architecture.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Authentication vs. authorization — which one does a session cookie prove?","a":"<p>Authentication only. Authorization must be checked per request against the specific resource.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why do parameterized queries prevent SQL injection?","a":"<p>The SQL and the values travel separately, so input is always treated as a value and can never change the query's structure.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Encryption in transit vs. at rest — does one imply the other?","a":"<p>No. TLS protects data on the network; at-rest encryption protects stored data if disks/backups are stolen. You need both.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is \"Base64 in a Kubernetes Secret\" not encryption?","a":"<p>Base64 is reversible encoding. Protection comes from RBAC on Secrets and encryption at rest in etcd (or an external secrets manager).</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How does XSS relate to SQL injection?","a":"<p>Same shape: untrusted input lands in an executable context — the browser instead of the database. Fix with context-aware output encoding, plus CSP as defense in depth.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is STRIDE used for?","a":"<p>A checklist for threat modeling: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege — walked against each component and trust boundary before building.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is SSRF and why is it severe in the cloud?","a":"<p>The server is tricked into requesting an attacker-chosen URL. In the cloud that can reach the instance metadata endpoint and steal temporary credentials. Mitigate with destination allowlists, blocking internal ranges, and IMDSv2.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why is key management as important as the encryption algorithm?","a":"<p>If the key sits next to the data or is readable by everyone, encryption protects nothing. Who can use the key, where it lives (KMS/HSM), and how it's rotated determine real protection.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How would you protect a login endpoint beyond hashing passwords?","a":"<p>Per-account and per-IP rate limits, lockout/backoff, MFA, breached-password checks, generic error messages, and alerts on failed-login spikes.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"An AWS access key was pushed to a public GitHub repo 10 minutes ago. What do you do, in order?","a":"<p>Deactivate/rotate the key immediately; check CloudTrail for its usage and contain anything it created; then purge it from history; then fix the process (secret scanning, roles instead of long-lived keys).</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A bug report says changing /invoices/1001 to /invoices/1002 shows another company's invoice. Fix and prevent?","a":"<p>Scope the lookup to the caller's tenant/user (or check permission explicitly) and return 404/403 otherwise; audit all ID-based endpoints; add authorization tests; consider non-guessable IDs as defense in depth (not a fix by itself).</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A dependency scanner flags a critical CVE. How do you decide urgency?","a":"<p>Check whether the vulnerable code path is reachable and exposed, whether exploits exist, and what data is at risk; patch fast for reachable, internet-facing paths, schedule otherwise — but don't let \"not reachable\" become permanent.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"observability-security/02-cicd-and-deployment-strategies.html","title":"CI/CD & Deployment Strategies","section":"Observability, Security & Ops","icon":"🛡️","cards":[{"q":"What Is CI/CD?","a":"<p>\"CI means every change is automatically built and tested on push. Continuous delivery means every passing change is packaged into an artifact that's always ready to release, and continuous deployment goes further and ships it to production automatically. The pipeline builds one immutable artifact, tests and scans it, deploys it with a strategy like rolling, blue-green, or canary, and verifies it with monitoring. Feature flags let me deploy code dark and turn features on separately, and I plan the rollback before I deploy.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Continuous delivery vs. continuous deployment?","a":"<p>Same pipeline; delivery keeps every change releasable but a human approves production, deployment ships automatically when checks pass.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why should the pipeline build one immutable artifact?","a":"<p>So the exact thing tested is the thing deployed, and rollback means redeploying a known previous artifact.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why is the testing pyramid shaped that way?","a":"<p>Unit tests are fast, cheap and precise, so most coverage goes there; integration tests catch wiring bugs; e2e tests are slow and flaky, so keep only the critical flows.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"When would you pick blue-green over canary?","a":"<p>Blue-green when you want an instant, all-at-once cutover and rollback and can afford double capacity; canary when the change's risk is unknown and you want to limit blast radius with real traffic.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What constraint does a rolling deploy put on your code?","a":"<p>Old and new versions serve traffic simultaneously, so APIs, message formats and schema must be compatible across both versions.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How do feature flags change rollback?","a":"<p>A bad feature is turned off by flipping the flag — no redeploy. Deploy and release become separate decisions.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why can't blue-green always roll back instantly?","a":"<p>Destructive migrations plus new-shape writes mean the old version may not run against the database any more. Use expand-and-contract migrations.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How would you automate canary analysis?","a":"<p>Compare the canary's SLIs (error ratio, p99) against the baseline over fixed windows with thresholds; promote on pass, auto-rollback on breach, and require enough traffic for the comparison to be meaningful.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"What makes a CI/CD pipeline a security risk, and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>It holds credentials that can deploy to production and executes third-party code. Use short-lived OIDC credentials, least privilege, pinned/signed dependencies and images, and protected branches.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Error rate doubles 3 minutes after a deploy. What do you do first?","a":"<p>Roll back (or flip the flag) immediately to restore service, then investigate with the deploy diff, traces and logs. Restore first, debug second.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"You need to rename a heavily used column with zero downtime. Plan it.","a":"<p>Expand: add the new column and dual-write, backfill. Switch reads to the new column. Contract: stop writing the old one, drop it in a later release once no version uses it. Each step is independently rollback-safe.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Deploys pass staging but fail in production regularly. What do you investigate?","a":"<p>Whether the same artifact digest is promoted, config/secret drift between environments, production-only dependencies or data volume, and whether staging tests cover the failing paths.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"ejpt/01a-network-reconnaissance.html","title":"Network Reconnaissance During Penetration Testing","section":"Penetration Testing (eJPT)","icon":"⚔️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Network Reconnaissance?","a":"<p>\"Network reconnaissance is mapping the network before testing it. I check my interfaces and subnets with ip addr, where my traffic goes with ip route, whether hosts answer with ping, how names resolve with getent hosts, and whether ports complete a TCP handshake with nc -vz — then move on to Nmap service enumeration. The goal is to understand the attack surface and see whether real network access matches what the architecture intends, not to attack straight away.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What is the difference between IP addressing and routing?","a":"<p>IP addressing identifies a network interface. Routing determines where packets should be forwarded to reach their destination.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Does a failed ping mean the server is down?","a":"<p>No. ICMP may be blocked while TCP services remain accessible.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is the difference between ARP and DNS?","a":"<p>ARP resolves IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses on a local network. DNS resolves hostnames to IP addresses or other DNS records.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What happens when you execute nc -vz 192.168.1.10 443?","a":"<p>Netcat attempts to establish a TCP connection with port 443. It reports whether the connection succeeds, is refused, or times out.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is the difference between an open and filtered port in Nmap?","a":"<p>An open port has an application accepting connections. A filtered port cannot be reliably classified because packet filtering prevents Nmap from determining whether it is open or closed.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why might Netdiscover fail inside Docker?","a":"<p>Because ARP operates within Layer 2 broadcast domains. Separate Docker bridge networks isolate broadcast traffic, and network namespaces provide additional isolation.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why use getent hosts instead of only nslookup when checking resolution on a target box?","a":"<p><code>getent</code> follows the Name Service Switch order in <code>/etc/nsswitch.conf</code> (e.g. <code>hosts: files dns</code>), so it sees <code>/etc/hosts</code> entries exactly as applications on that machine do; <code>nslookup</code> only queries DNS.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"From a compromised-in-scope app server, nc -vz -w 3 10.0.3.25 5432 succeeds against the database. Is that a finding?","a":"<p>Only if that server should not reach the database. The backend that owns the database is expected to connect; an unrelated server reaching 5432 suggests an overly permissive security group, incorrect segmentation, excessive database access, or a violation of least-privilege network architecture.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"nc to a port reports \"Connection timed out\" while another port reports \"Connection refused\". What does each tell you?","a":"<p>Refused means the destination was reachable enough to return a rejection — usually nothing is listening, or a firewall actively rejected it. Timed out means no reply within the timeout — a firewall may be silently dropping traffic, but routing problems or an unavailable target are also possible, so it is not conclusive.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"ejpt/01b-recon-command-list.html","title":"Network Reconnaissance Command List","section":"Penetration Testing (eJPT)","icon":"⚔️","cards":[{"q":"What Is the Network Reconnaissance Command Workflow?","a":"<p>\"I go from local to remote, cheapest first: ip addr and ip route to know my networks and path, ping and ARP for reachability, getent and dig for DNS, nc for a single port, then Nmap for hosts, ports and service versions. If results look wrong, I check ss, traceroute and tcpdump to see what actually happened on the wire.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What is an IP address?","a":"<p>A logical address assigned to a network interface that identifies where that interface sits in an IP network. One machine can have several (for example <code>eth0</code>, <code>docker0</code>, <code>tun0</code>), one per interface.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is CIDR and subnetting?","a":"<p>CIDR notation (<code>/24</code>) says how many leading bits of an address are the network portion; the rest are host bits. A <code>/24</code> has 8 host bits: 256 addresses, 254 traditionally usable. Subnetting is splitting a network into smaller ranges by using a longer prefix.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is a default gateway?","a":"<p>The next hop that receives traffic for any destination not matched by a more specific route — usually the local router. In <code>ip route</code> it is the <code>default via …</code> line.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is routing?","a":"<p>The decision of which interface and next-hop gateway a packet uses to reach its destination, made from the routing table. Same-subnet destinations go out directly; others go to a gateway.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is ARP?","a":"<p>Address Resolution Protocol: on an IPv4 local network it finds the MAC address for an IP address by broadcasting \"Who has X?\" and receiving \"X is at MAC\". It works only inside one Layer 2 segment.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is ICMP?","a":"<p>Internet Control Message Protocol: a network-layer protocol for errors and diagnostics, with no port numbers. <code>ping</code> sends ICMP Echo Requests and waits for Echo Replies.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is DNS resolution?","a":"<p>Translating a hostname into an IP address. On Linux the order of sources comes from NSS (<code>/etc/nsswitch.conf</code>), typically <code>/etc/hosts</code> first, then the DNS servers listed in <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is the TCP three-way handshake?","a":"<p>How TCP opens a connection: the client sends SYN, the server answers SYN-ACK, the client replies ACK. A successful <code>nc -vz</code> or <code>nmap -sT</code> result means this handshake completed.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is the difference between TCP and UDP?","a":"<p>TCP is connection-oriented: a handshake first, then reliable, ordered delivery. UDP is connectionless: no handshake and no delivery guarantee. That is why UDP ports are harder to scan — silence can mean open or filtered.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is a port?","a":"<p>A number (0–65535) that identifies a service endpoint on a host. The IP picks the machine; the port picks the service on it — e.g. 22 for SSH, 443 for HTTPS, 5432 for PostgreSQL.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is the difference between open, closed and filtered ports?","a":"<p>Open: an application accepts connections (the handshake completes). Closed: the host is reachable but answers with a rejection — nothing is listening. Filtered: no usable answer, usually because a firewall drops the probes, so the scanner cannot tell open from closed.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is the difference between Netdiscover and Nmap?","a":"<p>Netdiscover only uses ARP, so it finds hosts (IP and MAC) on your own Layer 2 segment. Nmap works across routed networks, discovers hosts with several probe types and also scans ports, services and versions.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Ping to a lab host shows 100% packet loss, but you suspect SSH is running. Why can ping fail even when a service is accessible?","a":"<p>The host, a firewall, a network ACL or a security group can block ICMP while still allowing TCP to port 22. Confirm with <code>nc -vz -w 3 TARGET_IP 22</code> instead of concluding the host is down.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Why does Docker networking affect host discovery?","a":"<p>Each container has its own network namespace, and each Docker bridge is its own Layer 2 segment. ARP broadcasts don't cross bridges or routers, so ARP tools like Netdiscover can't see containers on another bridge — use IP-based discovery and check the networks with <code>docker network inspect</code>.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"nc to a port keeps timing out. How does tcpdump help troubleshoot network communication?","a":"<p>It shows the packets that actually go over the wire instead of a tool's summary. With <code>tcpdump -i any host TARGET_IP</code> running, retry the check: SYNs leaving with no reply means something on the path drops them; a reset coming back means the port is closed; no SYNs at all means your own routing sends them elsewhere.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"web-servers/server.html","title":"Web Servers","section":"Web Servers","icon":"🖧","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Web Server vs. an Application Server?","a":"<p>\"A web server like Nginx handles HTTP itself — connections, TLS, static files, reverse proxying, and load balancing — while an application server runs the application code. Node, Go, .NET, and Rust include a production HTTP server in the runtime; Python, Ruby, and PHP need a separate app server like Gunicorn, Puma, or PHP-FPM. In production I put Nginx or a load balancer in front of either kind, so the app processes only handle dynamic requests.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What problem does a reverse proxy solve that the application can't easily solve itself?","a":"<p>It takes the connection-heavy work off expensive app workers: TLS termination, static files, buffering slow clients, request-size and rate limits, and spreading traffic over several instances. App workers then only ever see complete, ready-to-run requests.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why does a Django app need Gunicorn, while a Go app doesn't need an app server?","a":"<p>Go's standard library includes a production-grade HTTP server, so the binary is its own server. Django only ships a development server; in production a WSGI/ASGI server (Gunicorn, Uvicorn) owns the sockets and worker processes and calls Django through the WSGI/ASGI interface.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What's the difference between a 502 and a 504?","a":"<p>502 Bad Gateway: the proxy couldn't get a valid response — connection refused or reset, wrong address, crashed worker. 504 Gateway Timeout: the upstream accepted the request but didn't answer within <code>proxy_read_timeout</code>. 502 = broken or unreachable, 504 = too slow.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Is a reverse proxy the same thing as a load balancer?","a":"<p>No. A reverse proxy fronts an application (TLS, static files, forwarding). A load balancer spreads requests across several instances. Nginx can do both, but one proxy in front of one app server is not load balancing.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why can one Nginx worker hold thousands of connections when a Gunicorn sync worker handles one request at a time?","a":"<p>Nginx workers use an event loop over non-blocking sockets (epoll/kqueue): they only touch a connection when it has data, so an idle or slow client costs memory, not a blocked process. A sync Gunicorn worker runs one request start to finish, including any waiting on the database.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Which headers must a reverse proxy set, and what breaks without each?","a":"<ul><li><code>Host</code> — otherwise the app sees the upstream name; host validation fails or URLs are wrong.</li>\n          <li><code>X-Forwarded-For</code> / <code>X-Real-IP</code> — otherwise every client looks like 127.0.0.1.</li>\n          <li><code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> — otherwise the app thinks it's on HTTP: redirect loops and insecure links.</li></ul>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does Nginx buffer requests and responses by default?","a":"<p>So the app worker is held only for the time it takes to compute the response, not for as long as a slow client takes to upload or download. Nginx absorbs the slow network side. Disable response buffering only for streaming (SSE, long polling).</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How does nginx -s reload apply a new config without dropping connections?","a":"<p>The master validates the new config, starts new workers with it, then tells the old workers to stop accepting connections and exit after finishing in-flight requests. An invalid config is rejected and the old workers keep running.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Requests that take about 40 seconds return 502, not 504. Why?","a":"<p>Gunicorn's worker <code>--timeout</code> (30 s by default) is shorter than Nginx's <code>proxy_read_timeout</code> (60 s). Gunicorn kills the silent worker first, the connection closes, and Nginx reports \"upstream prematurely closed connection\" → 502. Fix the slow request and make inner timeouts ≤ outer ones.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How can X-Forwarded-For be spoofed, and how do you get the real client IP?","a":"<p>The client can send its own <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>; proxies append to it. Trust only the right-most entries added by proxies you control (configure the number of trusted hops, e.g. <code>ProxyFix(x_for=1)</code> or Nginx's <code>real_ip</code> module with <code>set_real_ip_from</code>), and never the left-most value.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Why enable upstream keepalive, and what does it require?","a":"<p>Without it, Nginx opens a new connection to the upstream for every request — extra latency and, over TCP, piles of <code>TIME_WAIT</code> sockets and ephemeral-port exhaustion at high rates. It needs <code>keepalive N</code> in the <code>upstream</code> block plus <code>proxy_http_version 1.1</code> and <code>proxy_set_header Connection \"\"</code>.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"In Kubernetes, where does the web-server role go?","a":"<p>To the Ingress controller (often Nginx-based) or a cloud load balancer, which terminates TLS and routes by host/path to Services. The app server (Gunicorn, Node) runs inside the Pod. The layering is the same; the proxy is now shared cluster infrastructure configured through Ingress resources.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"After every deploy, users see 502s for about 10 seconds. What's happening and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The app server is restarting, and Nginx forwards requests to a socket nobody is listening on yet (connection refused → 502). Fix: graceful restarts (Gunicorn reloads workers on <code>HUP</code>), or run several instances and do a rolling deploy behind health checks so traffic only reaches ready instances; <code>proxy_next_upstream</code> lets Nginx retry another upstream for idempotent requests.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"Uploads larger than 1 MB fail, and the app logs show nothing. What do you check?","a":"<p>Nginx's <code>client_max_body_size</code> (1 MB default) — Nginx returns 413 before the request ever reaches the app, which is why the app logs are empty. Raise it for the upload location only.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"You enabled \"force HTTPS\" in the app and now the site redirect-loops. Why?","a":"<p>TLS terminates at Nginx, so the app receives plain HTTP and keeps redirecting to HTTPS. Send <code>X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme</code> from Nginx and tell the app to trust it (<code>SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER</code> in Django, <code>ProxyFix</code> in Flask).</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"p99 latency jumps during peak traffic, but app CPU is only 30%. What do you suspect and check?","a":"<p>Worker saturation: all sync workers are busy waiting on I/O (database, external APIs), so new requests queue. Compare <code>$request_time</code> with <code>$upstream_response_time</code>, check worker utilisation and DB pool waits, and look for slow downstream calls in traces. Fix with timeouts on outbound calls, more workers/instances, or async workers.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"web-servers/wsgi.html","title":"WSGI Explained","section":"Web Servers","icon":"🖧","cards":[{"q":"What Is WSGI?","a":"<p>\"WSGI is the Python standard interface between a web server and a Python web application. A WSGI server like Gunicorn receives the request, calls the app's application(environ, start_response) callable, and returns the response, so any WSGI server can run any WSGI framework such as Django or Flask. In production, Nginx sits in front for TLS, static files, and proxying. WSGI is synchronous, one request per worker at a time; ASGI is the async successor used by FastAPI and for WebSockets.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"What is WSGI, and why do we need it?","a":"<p>A Python standard (PEP 3333) for how a server calls a web app: <code>application(environ, start_response)</code>. It decouples servers from frameworks, so any WSGI server runs any WSGI framework.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"How does an HTTP server differ from a WSGI server?","a":"<p>The HTTP server (Nginx) owns client connections, TLS and static files and forwards dynamic requests; the WSGI server (Gunicorn) manages Python worker processes and translates HTTP into WSGI calls.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Can Django run without WSGI?","a":"<p>Django needs some server interface: it ships both a WSGI entry point (<code>wsgi.py</code>) and, since Django 3.0, an ASGI one (<code>asgi.py</code>), so it can run under Uvicorn/Daphne instead. The <code>runserver</code> dev server is for development only.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Explain the request flow from a browser to a Django app.","a":"<p>Browser → DNS/TCP/TLS to Nginx → Nginx serves static or proxies to Gunicorn → a Gunicorn worker builds <code>environ</code> and calls Django's WSGI app → middleware, URL routing, view, DB queries → response back through Gunicorn and Nginx.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why do we use Nginx in front of Gunicorn?","a":"<p>Sync Gunicorn workers would be held by slow clients; Nginx buffers them, terminates TLS, serves static files, enforces limits and load-balances, and keeps Gunicorn off the internet.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Difference between Gunicorn and uWSGI.","a":"<p>Gunicorn: pure-Python pre-fork server, few options, easy to run. uWSGI: written in C, extremely configurable (its own <code>uwsgi</code> protocol, many plugins), but complex, and the project has been in maintenance mode. Gunicorn is the usual default.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What is ASGI, and how is it different from WSGI?","a":"<p>ASGI is the async interface (<code>async app(scope, receive, send)</code>). WSGI blocks a worker per request; ASGI lets one event loop interleave many requests and supports WebSockets and long-lived connections.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"How would you scale a Django app running on Gunicorn?","a":"<p>Tune workers/threads per instance by measuring; run more stateless instances behind a load balancer; move slow work to a task queue; cache; put a connection pooler in front of the DB; serve static from a CDN.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"How does Nginx handle SSL termination in a Django deployment?","a":"<p>Nginx holds the certificate and does the TLS handshake, then proxies plain HTTP to Gunicorn with <code>X-Forwarded-Proto: https</code>. Django must trust that header (<code>SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER</code>) or it will think requests are HTTP — breaking secure cookies and redirects.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Can a single server handle both static and dynamic content? Pros/cons?","a":"<p>Yes (Django can serve static via WhiteNoise; Nginx can do both with a proxy). One process is simpler, but serving static through Python workers wastes expensive workers; Nginx or a CDN serves static far more efficiently.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Users see 502s only on the report-export endpoint, which takes ~45 s. Why?","a":"<p>Gunicorn's 30 s worker timeout kills the worker before Nginx's 60 s timeout; Nginx sees a closed connection → 502. Make export asynchronous (queue + download link) rather than raising timeouts.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"After scaling from 2 to 8 instances, the site gets slower and the DB logs connection errors. Why?","a":"<p>Total DB connections = instances × workers × pool size, now above the DB limit. Add PgBouncer or shrink pools; scale the web tier only within the DB's connection budget.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"aws/00-aws-learning-path.html","title":"AWS Learning Path","section":"AWS Learning","icon":"☁️","cards":[{"q":"What Is AWS?","a":"<p>\"AWS is Amazon's public cloud: it rents servers, storage, databases and networking on demand, billed by usage, so I don't have to buy and size hardware up front. It runs in Regions, each with multiple isolated Availability Zones, and I get high availability by spreading my workload across AZs. Security is shared — AWS secures the physical infrastructure, and I secure my IAM permissions, network rules, data, and anything I run on EC2. For a typical web app I'd use Route 53 and CloudFront at the edge, an Application Load Balancer, EC2 or containers in private subnets across two AZs, RDS Multi-AZ for the database, S3 for files, and CloudWatch for monitoring.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"aws/01-elasticache.html","title":"Amazon ElastiCache","section":"AWS Learning","icon":"☁️","cards":[{"q":"What Is Amazon ElastiCache?","a":"<p>\"ElastiCache is AWS's managed in-memory cache running Valkey, Redis OSS, or Memcached. I put it in front of RDS, Aurora, or DynamoDB for microsecond reads using lazy loading with TTLs. Serverless is the default when I don't want to size anything — it scales automatically and bills per GB and per ECPU; node-based clusters give me control over node types, shards, and replicas. For HA I use Valkey or Redis OSS with Multi-AZ and automatic failover, cluster mode for sharding, and Global Datastore for cross-Region reads and disaster recovery. It runs inside my VPC, secured with security groups, TLS, encryption at rest, and IAM or RBAC authentication.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"software-development-principles/01-sdp.html","title":"Software Development Principles","section":"Dev Principles","icon":"🧭","cards":[{"q":"What Are Design Patterns?","a":"<p>\"Design patterns are named, reusable solutions to common object-oriented design problems, popularized by the Gang of Four book's 23 patterns. They fall into three groups: creational patterns like Factory and Builder control object creation, structural patterns like Adapter, Decorator, and Facade control how objects are composed, and behavioral patterns like Strategy, Observer, and Command control how objects interact. Their value is proven trade-offs and a shared vocabulary, and most of them rest on programming to interfaces and preferring composition over inheritance.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"path/00-system-desing.html","title":"System Design: EC2 Hosting Checklist","section":"Planning & Roadmap","icon":"🗺️","cards":[{"q":"How Would You Design Hosting for a Website on EC2?","a":"<p>\"I'd start from requirements — traffic, uptime SLA, compliance. Then I'd put the app on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling Group across at least two Availability Zones, behind an Application Load Balancer, inside a VPC with the instances and an RDS Multi-AZ database in private subnets. Static files go in S3 behind CloudFront, DNS in Route 53, TLS certificates from ACM. I'd use IAM roles, Secrets Manager, security groups, and WAF for security; Terraform and CI/CD with rolling or blue-green deploys; CloudWatch for monitoring; automated backups with a clear RPO and RTO; and right-sizing and Savings Plans for cost.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"path/01-requirements-and-planning.html","title":"Requirements & Planning","section":"Planning & Roadmap","icon":"🗺️","cards":[{"q":"What Are Functional and Non-Functional Requirements?","a":"<p>\"Before designing, I clarify requirements. Functional requirements are what the system does — the features, user flows, and APIs. Non-functional requirements are how well it does it — latency, throughput, availability, scalability, security, and compliance. I also estimate scale: daily users, peak requests per second, read/write ratio, and data growth. The functional requirements define the APIs and data model, and the non-functional ones drive the architecture — caching, replication, multi-AZ, and so on.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"path/01-b-MVP.html","title":"MVP Planning","section":"Planning & Roadmap","icon":"🗺️","cards":[{"q":"What Is an MVP (Minimum Viable Product)?","a":"<p>\"An MVP is the smallest working version of a product that delivers its core value to real users, so we can validate the idea and learn before building more. For system design, that means designing only for the core flow and realistic launch traffic, using simple managed services like API Gateway, Lambda, S3, SQS, and a managed database, and keeping the design easy to evolve once real usage tells us where it needs to scale.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"path/02-apis.html","title":"API Planning","section":"Planning & Roadmap","icon":"🗺️","cards":[{"q":"How Do You Choose Between REST, GraphQL, and gRPC?","a":"<p>\"I default to REST for public and CRUD APIs because it's simple, universally supported, and works with HTTP caching. I choose GraphQL when clients like mobile apps or SPAs need flexible, precisely shaped data from many sources in one request. I use gRPC for internal service-to-service calls where performance, strict typed contracts, and streaming matter. In every case I define the schema, the auth model, consistent error handling, and a versioning strategy up front.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"project-explaintion/image-share-system/200-images.html","title":"Image Share System (200 Images)","section":"Project Walkthroughs","icon":"🏗️","cards":[{"q":"How Do You Serve 200 Random Images per Request at Scale?","a":"<p>\"Picking 200 random images from the database on every request doesn't scale, so I precompute. A background worker builds many random sets of 200 image IDs, with signed CDN URLs already generated, and stores each set as one JSON value in Redis with a short TTL. A request just goes through the load balancer, rate limiter, and auth, reads one precomputed set from Redis, and returns it; the client then downloads the images directly from the CDN, which is backed by S3. Metadata lives in RDS, image bytes in S3, and a real-time fallback plus a circuit breaker handle cache misses and failures.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"project-explaintion/instagram/major-tools.html","title":"Instagram: Major Tools","section":"Project Walkthroughs","icon":"🏗️","cards":[{"q":"What Does an Instagram-Scale Architecture Look Like?","a":"<p>\"Instagram-scale systems use the right tool for each job. Clients call a GraphQL API backed by Django services. PostgreSQL holds relational data like users and posts, Cassandra holds high-volume writes like likes and follows, and Memcached caches hot reads. Media goes to object storage behind a CDN via presigned uploads. RabbitMQ handles background tasks and Kafka streams events into Spark, Flink, and ML models that rank the feed. It all runs in containers on Kubernetes, with CI/CD, Prometheus and Grafana metrics, and centralized logging.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"project-explaintion/chat-system/chat-architecture-review.html","title":"Chat Architecture Review: Postgres + PubNub","section":"Project Walkthroughs","icon":"🏗️","cards":[{"q":"What Is a \"Database as Source of Truth + Pub/Sub for Delivery\" Chat Architecture?","a":"<p>\"I save every message to Postgres first — that's the source of truth — and only after the commit do I publish a small event to PubNub so online clients update instantly. PubNub is just the delivery pipe, so if it goes down nobody loses messages; they just stop getting live updates until they reconnect and re-fetch from the API. To make that safe I give each user a scoped PubNub token, write the publish event into an outbox table in the same transaction, dedupe client retries with a client-generated message ID and a unique constraint, and page message history with a cursor instead of an offset.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"mislanious/01-pubsub-and-pubnub.html","title":"Pub/Sub and PubNub","section":"Miscellaneous","icon":"🧩","cards":[{"q":"What Is Pub/Sub, and How Is PubNub Different?","a":"<p>\"Pub/sub is a messaging pattern where publishers send messages to a topic and every subscriber to that topic receives a copy, through a broker, so senders and receivers don't know about each other. Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis, SNS, and Google Pub/Sub implement it. PubNub is a managed real-time platform built on that pattern: it gives client apps SDKs, global low-latency delivery, presence, and history, so I don't run WebSocket infrastructure. I'd use SNS or Kafka for backend events, and PubNub for pushing live updates to users' devices.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"mislanious/Memory-Profiling.html","title":"Memory Profiling","section":"Miscellaneous","icon":"🧩","cards":[{"q":"What Is Memory Profiling?","a":"<p>\"Memory profiling is analyzing how a program uses memory while it runs — what allocates memory, how it grows over time, and what is still holding references. I use it to find memory leaks and reduce memory usage. The usual approach is to take heap snapshots or allocation traces at two points, compare them, and find the objects that keep growing, with tools like tracemalloc in Python, VisualVM in Java, Valgrind in C++, or Chrome DevTools for Node.js.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"mislanious/query-string.html","title":"Query Strings","section":"Miscellaneous","icon":"🧩","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Query String?","a":"<p>\"A query string is the part of the URL after the question mark, with key-value pairs separated by ampersands, like ?page=2&amp;sort=price. It lets one endpoint handle search, filtering, sorting, pagination, and tracking without a separate URL for each variation. The server parses it and uses the values, for example in a database query, and load balancers like AWS ALB can even route on it. I use the path to identify a resource and the query string to filter or shape the result.\"</p>","l":"core"}]},
{"href":"books/domain-driven-design/01-crunching-knowledge.html","title":"Domain-Driven Design, Ch 1 — Crunching Knowledge","section":"Books","icon":"📚","cards":[{"q":"What Is Knowledge Crunching in Domain-Driven Design?","a":"<p>\"Knowledge crunching is how developers and domain experts build a domain model together. Instead of taking requirements one way, they sketch models, test them against real scenarios and early prototypes, and keep refining — adding the concepts that matter, dropping the ones that don't, and using the same language in code and conversation. The result is a model that captures business rules explicitly, like an overbooking policy as its own object rather than a magic number, and the team keeps learning so the model gets deeper over time.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why can't developers just get the domain knowledge from a requirements document?","a":"<p>Because experts' knowledge is scattered, partly tacit and full of exceptions they apply without noticing. A document captures what someone thought to write down; the model has to be discovered through back-and-forth, where developers' questions and prototypes force experts to make their reasoning explicit.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What are the five ingredients of effective modeling in Chapter 1?","a":"<p>Binding the model and the implementation; cultivating a language based on the model; developing a knowledge-rich model; distilling the model; and brainstorming and experimenting.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Why does a waterfall analyst → developer handoff produce a weak model?","a":"<p>Knowledge flows one way. Analysts never learn what the implementation reveals, and developers never absorb the domain, so nobody corrects the model with experience. The software ends up implementing the analysts' first understanding, gaps included.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does Evans extract an OverbookingPolicy class instead of leaving the * 1.1 check in place?","a":"<p>The check is an important business rule that experts talk about by name. As a magic number inside a guard clause, it is invisible to readers and impossible for experts to verify. As a named policy object it is explicit, findable, testable, changeable in one place — and a domain expert can read the calling code and confirm it.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What distinguishes a deep model from a superficial one, and how do you get one?","a":"<p>A superficial model mirrors the visible surface (in shipping: ships, containers, loading). A deep model captures what the business fundamentally reasons about (transfer of responsibility for cargo between parties). You reach it through continued knowledge crunching and refactoring after experience with real cases; a sign you're there is that new requirements fall out of the model instead of needing special cases.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Isn't making every rule an object over-engineering?","a":"<p>Yes, if done for every detail — and Evans says so. The technique is reserved for rules that matter to the business and that experts discuss. Trivial validation can stay inline; core policies deserve explicit names.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Business says \"the overbooking limit is now 15% on Asia routes\". The change took a week and broke billing. What went wrong, and what would you change?","a":"<p>The rule was implicit — a magic number duplicated across booking, billing and reporting, probably already inconsistent. Find every occurrence (search for the literal), extract one explicit policy concept (an <code>OverbookingPolicy</code> that can vary by route), route all call sites through it, and confirm the new model with the business. Going forward, model every rule experts name as an explicit concept.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"The only engineer who understands the pricing module is leaving. What does Chapter 1 suggest?","a":"<p>This is a continuous-learning failure: knowledge lives in a person, not in the model. Before they leave, pair them with others on refactoring pricing toward explicit, named concepts in the experts' language, and bring a pricing expert into those sessions. The knowledge then survives in code and shared vocabulary.</p>","l":"scenario"}]},
{"href":"books/domain-driven-design/02-communication-and-language.html","title":"Domain-Driven Design, Ch 2 — Communication and the Use of Language","section":"Books","icon":"📚","cards":[{"q":"What Is a Ubiquitous Language?","a":"<p>\"A Ubiquitous Language is one shared vocabulary, based on the domain model, that developers and business experts both use — in meetings, tickets, diagrams and directly in code names. It removes the translation between business and technical terms, which is where requirements get lost. If the team changes a term, that's a model change, so the code gets renamed too. In a large system each bounded context has its own language.\"</p>","l":"core"},{"q":"Why is translating between business terms and technical terms harmful?","a":"<p>Each translation loses precision. Experts can't verify the design because it's described in terms they don't use, developers implement their own interpretation, and different people invent different names for the same concept — so misunderstandings become bugs.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"Where should the Ubiquitous Language appear?","a":"<p>Everywhere the team communicates: conversation, tickets and acceptance criteria, diagrams, documents, test names, and class and method names in code.</p>","l":"basic"},{"q":"What is \"modeling out loud\" and why does it work?","a":"<p>Describing scenarios aloud in model terms. People quickly notice when spoken sentences are convoluted; an awkward sentence usually means a concept is missing or misnamed. Refining the sentence (e.g. to \"a Routing Service finds an Itinerary that satisfies a Route Specification\") refines the model.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"Why does Evans say a change in the language is a change in the model?","a":"<p>The language is built on the model, so renaming or splitting a term changes what concepts exist and how they relate. If the code keeps the old names, the team is back to translating between how it talks and how the code is written.</p>","l":"intermediate"},{"q":"What role should UML diagrams and design documents play in DDD?","a":"<p>A supporting one. Diagrams show structure well but not meaning or behaviour, so keep them small and pair them with text. Documents should complement code and speech (explain concepts and decisions) and stay current by being used; the code is the precise, executable statement of the design. Explanatory models can teach the domain without matching the code.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"What does it mean if domain experts can't understand your model?","a":"<p>Evans treats it as a sign the model is wrong — it is probably expressed in technical terms or doesn't capture the domain's real concepts. Sophisticated experts should be able to follow and challenge the model.</p>","l":"advanced"},{"q":"Billing and shipping teams keep arguing about what a \"customer\" is, and integration bugs follow. What do you do?","a":"<p>Recognise that the word has two legitimate meanings in two models. Don't force one definition; give each area its own precise term within its own context (a Bounded Context, Ch 14) and make the mapping between them explicit at the integration point.</p>","l":"scenario"},{"q":"A new developer finds Shipment, Consignment and CargoRecord classes that seem to be the same thing. What's the fix?","a":"<p>Ask the domain experts which term they use and whether these are truly one concept. If they are, pick the agreed term and rename across code, tests and tickets. If they aren't, define the difference precisely and make it visible in the model.</p>","l":"scenario"}]}
];
